  $$d = \frac{1}{2}at^2 \implies t = \sqrt{\frac{2d}{a}} = \sqrt{\frac{2(0.30\text{ m})}{0.25\text{ m/s}^2}} = \sqrt{2.4} \approx 1.55\text{ s}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q3', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 3, false, 'D', 'Electric Field Superposition & Tripling', 'medium', 'Points A and B are equidistant from point P. At A, a charge $+Q$ creates field $\vec{E}$ pointing to the right at P. What charge placed at B will triple the net electric field at P?', '["$+2Q$","$+3Q$","$-Q$","$-2Q$","$-3Q$"]'::jsonb, '(D) To amplify the field at P to $3E$ in the same direction, the charge at B must contribute an electric field of magnitude $2E$ directed toward B (to the right). A negative charge attracts a positive test charge, so the charge must be negative with twice the magnitude: $q_B = -2Q$.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q4', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 4, false, 'D', 'Repulsive Coulomb Force Kinematics', 'easy', 'A fixed sphere $+Q$ repels a small movable sphere $+q$ released from rest. The small sphere moves away with:', '["decreasing velocity and decreasing acceleration","decreasing velocity and increasing acceleration","decreasing velocity and constant acceleration","increasing velocity and decreasing acceleration","increasing velocity and increasing acceleration"]'::jsonb, '(D) As $r$ increases, the repulsive force $F = \frac{kQq}{r^2}$ and acceleration $a = F/m$ decrease toward zero. Because acceleration remains strictly positive ($a > 0$), the speed and velocity continue to increase ($dv/dt > 0$).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q5', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 5, false, 'A', 'Electrostatic Potential Energy Turnaround', 'hard', 'A particle of charge $q = 1\ \mu\text{C}$ is fixed at the origin. An object ($m = 10^{-3}\text{ kg}, q = 1\ \mu\text{C}$) is released from rest $1\text{ m}$ directly above it. How far does the object fall before coming momentarily to rest ($g = 10\text{ m/s}^2$)?', '["$0.1\\text{ m}$","$0.3\\text{ m}$","$0.5\\text{ m}$","$0.7\\text{ m}$","$0.9\\text{ m}$"]'::jsonb, '(A) At the turnaround point (fall distance $x$), $\Delta K = 0$, so net work done by gravity and electrostatic repulsion is zero:
  $$W_g + W_E = 0 \implies mgx - k q^2 \left(\frac{1}{1-x} - 1\right) = 0 \implies mgx = \frac{k q^2 x}{1-x}$$
  $$1 - x = \frac{k q^2}{mg} = \frac{(9 \times 10^9)(10^{-6})^2}{(10^{-3})(10)} = \frac{9 \times 10^{-3}}{10^{-2}} = 0.9 \implies x = 1 - 0.9 = 0.1\text{ m}$$')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

  $$E(4\pi r^2) = \frac{Q(r^3/R^3)}{\varepsilon_0} \implies E(r) = \frac{Q r}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 R^3}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q8', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 8, false, 'A', 'Gaussian Surface Enclosing Electric Dipole', 'easy', 'Calculate the electric flux through a Gaussian surface enclosing an electric dipole of charge magnitude $q$.', '["$0$","$\\frac{Aq}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}$","$\\frac{Aq^2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}$","$\\frac{Aq}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r}$","$\\frac{Aq}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^2}$"]'::jsonb, '(A) A dipole consists of equal and opposite charges $+q$ and $-q$. Total enclosed net charge $Q_{\text{enc}} = +q + (-q) = 0$. By Gauss''s Law, $\Phi_E = \frac{Q_{\text{enc}}}{\varepsilon_0} = 0$.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q9', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 9, false, 'C', 'Equipotential Work & Potential Difference', 'medium', 'An electron moves from point A ($-10\text{ V}$) to E ($-10\text{ V}$), then from E to C ($0\text{ V}$). Describe the work done by the electric field for each step.', '["Negative; Positive","Zero; Positive","Zero; Negative","Positive; Zero","Positive; Positive"]'::jsonb, '(C) From A to E: $\Delta V = 0\text{ V}$ (equipotential line), so $W_E = -q\Delta V = 0\text{ J}$.  
From E to C: $\Delta V = 0 - (-10) = +10\text{ V}$. Work done by the electric field on the negative electron: $W_E = -q\Delta V = -(-e)(+10\text{ V}) = -10\text{ eV}$ (negative work).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q10', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 10, false, 'B', 'Work by Conservative Electric Field', 'easy', 'How much work does the electric field do moving charge $q = -0.05\text{ C}$ from $V_A = 200\text{ V}$ to $V_B = 100\text{ V}$ along a curved path?', '["$-5\\text{ J}$","$+5\\text{ J}$","$-10\\text{ J}$","$+10\\text{ J}$","$0\\text{ J}$"]'::jsonb, '(B) $$W_E = -q(V_B - V_A) = -(-0.05\text{ C})(100\text{ V} - 200\text{ V}) = +0.05(-100) \implies W_E = -\Delta U = +5\text{ J}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q11', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 11, false, 'E', 'Capacitor Dielectric Insertion & Stored Energy', 'easy', 'A capacitor is fully charged by a battery, disconnected, and then a dielectric is inserted. Which statements are true?  I. Voltage remains the same.  II. Potential energy increases.  III. Capacitance increases.', '["I only","I and II only","I and III only","II and III only","III only"]'::jsonb, '(E) With the battery disconnected, stored charge $Q$ is constant. Inserting a dielectric increases capacitance ($C = \kappa C_0$, III is true). Voltage decreases ($V = Q/C$, I is false), and stored energy decreases ($U = Q^2/(2C)$, II is false).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q12', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 12, false, 'C', 'Electric Potential Energy Sign Definition', 'easy', 'If the electric field does negative work on a negative charge moving from A to B, the electrical potential energy:', '["is negative","is positive","increases","decreases","cannot be determined"]'::jsonb, '(C) By definition, $\Delta U_E = -W_E$. If $W_E < 0$, then $\Delta U_E > 0$, meaning the potential energy increases.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q13', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 13, false, 'D', 'Parallel Capacitors Individual Stored Energy', 'easy', 'Three $6\ \mu\text{F}$ capacitors are connected in parallel across a $9\text{ V}$ battery. Determine the energy stored in each individual capacitor.', '["$2.43\\text{ J}$","$7.29 \\times 10^{-4}\\text{ J}$","$8.10 \\times 10^{-5}\\text{ J}$","$2.43 \\times 10^{-4}\\text{ J}$","$27\\text{ J}$"]'::jsonb, '(D) Each parallel capacitor receives the full $9\text{ V}$ potential difference:
  $$U = \frac{1}{2}C V^2 = \frac{1}{2}(6 \times 10^{-6}\text{ F})(9\text{ V})^2 = (3 \times 10^{-6})(81) = 2.43 \times 10^{-4}\text{ J}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q14', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 14, false, 'C', 'Equipotential Surfaces in Uniform Field', 'easy', 'In a uniform electric field between parallel plates, which points lie on the same equipotential?', '["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 4 only","3 and 4 only","All points lie on same equipotential"]'::jsonb, '(C) Equipotential surfaces in a uniform electric field are parallel planes perpendicular to the field lines. Points located at the same perpendicular distance from the plates share the identical electric potential.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q15', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 15, false, 'C', 'Connected Conducting Spheres Charge Equilibrium', 'medium', 'Isolated conducting spheres of radius $a$ (Sphere 1) and $4a$ (Sphere 2) each carry charge $-Q$. Connected by a thin wire, what are the final charges?', '["$-Q,\\ -Q$","$-2Q/3,\\ -4Q/3$","$-2Q/5,\\ -8Q/5$","$-4Q/5,\\ -6Q/5$","$-Q/2,\\ -3Q/2$"]'::jsonb, '(C) Total charge $Q_{\text{tot}} = -2Q$. When connected, potential equalizes: $V_1 = V_2 \implies \frac{k q_1}{a} = \frac{k q_2}{4a} \implies q_2 = 4q_1$.
  $$q_1 + 4q_1 = -2Q \implies 5q_1 = -2Q \implies q_1 = -\frac{2}{5}Q,\quad q_2 = -\frac{8}{5}Q$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q16', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 16, false, 'A', 'Resistivity & Conductor Cross-Section Ratio', 'easy', 'Brass wire has $4\times$ diameter and $5\times$ resistivity of silver wire of the same length. Compare resistances $R_B$ and $R_S$.', '["$R_B = \\frac{5}{16}R_S$","$R_B = \\frac{5}{4}R_S$","$R_B = \\frac{5}{64}R_S$","$R_B = \\frac{16}{5}R_S$","$R_B = 20 R_S$"]'::jsonb, '(A) Area scales with diameter squared: $A_B = 4^2 A_S = 16 A_S$.
  $$R_B = \frac{\rho_B L}{A_B} = \frac{(5\rho_S)L}{16 A_S} = \frac{5}{16}R_S$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q17', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 17, false, 'D', 'Ohm''s Law Proportionality', 'easy', 'Doubling the voltage across an ohmic conductor without changing resistance causes current to:', '["decrease by 4","decrease by 2","remain unchanged","increase by a factor of 2","increase by 4"]'::jsonb, '(D) By Ohm''s Law, $I = V/R$. For constant $R$, current is directly proportional to voltage, doubling when $V$ is doubled.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q18', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 18, false, 'C', 'Circuit Power & Operating Voltage', 'easy', 'A circuit with total resistance $5\ \Omega$ dissipates $125\text{ W}$ of power. At what voltage is it operating?', '["$5\\text{ V}$","$15\\text{ V}$","$25\\text{ V}$","$45\\text{ V}$","$625\\text{ V}$"]'::jsonb, '(C) $$P = \frac{V^2}{R} \implies V = \sqrt{P R} = \sqrt{(125\text{ W})(5\ \Omega)} = \sqrt{625} = 25\text{ V}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q19', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 19, false, 'A', 'Incandescent Bulb Overvoltage Power', 'easy', 'A $120\text{ V}, 60\text{ W}$ bulb is connected to a $240\text{ V}$ outlet. How bright is the bulb compared to standard operation?', '["4 times as bright","2 times as bright","Same brightness","Half as bright","One-quarter as bright"]'::jsonb, '(A) $$P = \frac{V^2}{R} \propto V^2$$
Doubling the operating voltage quadruples the power dissipated ($2^2 = 4$), making the bulb 4 times as bright.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q20', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 20, false, 'B', 'Ammeter Construction & Series Resistance', 'easy', 'An ammeter arranged to measure circuit branch current should possess which characteristics?', '["High resistance, in series","Low resistance, in series","High resistance, in parallel","Low resistance, in parallel","Same resistance, in series"]'::jsonb, '(B) Ammeters must be placed in series so the entire branch current passes through them. Their internal resistance must approach zero ($R \approx 0$) so they do not add series resistance or alter the circuit current.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q21', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 21, false, 'A', 'Magnetic Force Work & Trajectory Properties', 'easy', 'Which statements are true regarding magnetic forces?  I. Magnetic force can never do work on a charged particle.  II. Magnetic force can never change the velocity of a charged particle.  III. A charged particle always experiences a force moving in a magnetic field.', '["I only","I and II only","II and III only","III only","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '(A) $\vec{F}_B = q(\vec{v}\times\vec{B})$ is always perpendicular to $\vec{v}$, so power $P = \vec{F}_B\cdot\vec{v} = 0$ and work is always zero (I is true). It alters direction, thus changing velocity vector $\vec{v}$ (II is false). Moving parallel to $\vec{B}$ produces zero force (III is false).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q22', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 22, false, 'C', 'Magnetic Lorentz Force & Particle Acceleration', 'medium', 'Particle ($q = 4.0\text{ nC}, m = 2 \times 10^{-4}\text{ kg}$) moves perpendicular to $B = 0.1\text{ T}$ at $3 \times 10^4\text{ m/s}$. What is its acceleration?', '["$0.0006\\text{ m/s}^2$","$0.006\\text{ m/s}^2$","$0.06\\text{ m/s}^2$","$0.6\\text{ m/s}^2$","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '(C) $$F_B = q v B = (4.0 \times 10^{-9}\text{ C})(3 \times 10^4\text{ m/s})(0.1\text{ T}) = 1.2 \times 10^{-5}\text{ N}$$
$$a = \frac{F_B}{m} = \frac{1.2 \times 10^{-5}\text{ N}}{2 \times 10^{-4}\text{ kg}} = 0.06\text{ m/s}^2$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q23', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 23, false, 'D', 'Biot-Savart Law Straight Wire Magnetic Field', 'easy', 'Find the magnetic field $2\text{ cm}$ from a long straight wire carrying $10\text{ A}$.', '["$1 \\times 10^{-6}\\text{ T}$","$1 \\times 10^{-5}\\text{ T}$","$2 \\times 10^{-5}\\text{ T}$","$1 \\times 10^{-4}\\text{ T}$","$2 \\times 10^{-4}\\text{ T}$"]'::jsonb, '(D) $$B = \frac{\mu_0 I}{2\pi r} = \frac{(4\pi \times 10^{-7})(10)}{2\pi (0.02)} = \frac{4 \times 10^{-6}}{0.04} = 1.0 \times 10^{-4}\text{ T}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q24', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 24, false, 'E', 'Motional EMF & Endpoint Potential Polarity', 'medium', 'A metal rod of length $L$ is pulled upward with speed $v$ through a uniform magnetic field $B$ pointing out of the page. What is the potential difference between endpoints $a$ (left) and $b$ (right)?', '["$0$","$\\frac{1}{2}vBL$, $a$ higher","$\\frac{1}{2}vBL$, $b$ higher","$vBL$, point $a$ at higher potential","$vBL$, point $b$ at higher potential"]'::jsonb, '(E) By the right-hand rule, $\vec{v}\times\vec{B}$ for upward velocity and out-of-page field points to the right. Mobile electrons are pushed left toward point $a$, leaving positive charge accumulation at point $b$. Point $b$ is at the higher electric potential by $\Delta V = vBL$.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-drills_q25', 'ap-topic-physc-em-drills', 25, false, 'A', 'Motional EMF on Rails & Lenz''s Law Current', 'medium', 'A conducting rod of length $0.2\text{ m}$ and resistance $10\ \Omega$ slides at $3\text{ m/s}$ to the left along rails in $B = 0.5\text{ T}$ directed into the page. What is the magnitude and direction of the induced current?', '["$0.03\\text{ A}$, downward","$0.03\\text{ A}$, upward","$0.3\\text{ A}$, downward","$0.3\\text{ A}$, upward","$0.015\\text{ A}$, downward"]'::jsonb, '(A) $$\mathcal{E} = B L v = (0.5\text{ T})(0.2\text{ m})(3\text{ m/s}) = 0.3\text{ V}$$
$$I = \frac{\mathcal{E}}{R} = \frac{0.3\text{ V}}{10\ \Omega} = 0.03\text{ A}$$
Moving to the left decreases the loop area and inward flux. By Lenz''s Law, the induced current circulates clockwise to produce inward flux, flowing downward through the moving rod.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q1', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 1, false, 'C', 'Electrostatic Induction & Polarization', 'easy', 'A nonconducting sphere is given a nonzero net electric charge, $+Q$, and then brought close to a neutral conducting sphere of the same radius. Which of the following will be true?', '["An electric field will be induced within the conducting sphere.","The conducting sphere will develop a net electric charge of $-Q$.","The spheres will experience an electrostatic attraction.","The spheres will experience an electrostatic repulsion.","The spheres will experience no electrostatic interaction."]'::jsonb, '(C) The positive charge $+Q$ on the nonconducting sphere induces negative mobile electrons to migrate toward the near side of the neutral conducting sphere, leaving equal positive bound charge on the far side. Because electrostatic force varies inversely with the square of distance ($F \propto 1/r^2$), the attractive force on the closer negative charges is strictly greater than the repulsive force on the farther positive charges, resulting in a net attractive electrostatic force.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q2', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 2, false, 'E', 'Electric Power & Resistance Relationships', 'easy', 'If the total resistance of a circuit were doubled, the power dissipated by that same circuit would', '["increase by a factor of 4","increase by a factor of 2","decrease by a factor of 2","decrease by a factor of 4","Cannot be determined"]'::jsonb, '(E) Power dissipation can be expressed as $P = I^2 R$ or $P = V^2/R$. If the circuit is operated under a constant voltage source, doubling $R$ cuts power in half ($P \propto 1/R$). If operated under a constant current source, doubling $R$ doubles the power ($P \propto R$). Without specifying whether the voltage or current remains fixed, the change in power dissipation cannot be determined.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q3', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 3, false, 'E', 'Magnetic Lorentz Force & Orbital Radius', 'medium', 'Each of the following ionized isotopes is projected with the same speed into a uniform magnetic field $B$ such that the isotope''s initial velocity is perpendicular to $B$. Which combination of mass and charge would result in a circular path with the largest radius?', '["$m = 16\\text{ u},\\ q = -5e$","$m = 18\\text{ u},\\ q = -2e$","$m = 19\\text{ u},\\ q = -2e$","$m = 16\\text{ u},\\ q = +e$","$m = 20\\text{ u},\\ q = +e$"]'::jsonb, '(E) The magnetic Lorentz force supplies the centripetal acceleration:
  $$|q|vB = \frac{mv^2}{r} \implies r = \frac{mv}{|q|B}$$
Since $v$ and $B$ are identical for all particles, the radius $r$ is directly proportional to the mass-to-charge magnitude ratio $m/|q|$. Evaluating $m/|q|$:
(A) $16/5 = 3.2$
(B) $18/2 = 9.0$
(C) $19/2 = 9.5$
(D) $16/1 = 16.0$
(E) $20/1 = 20.0$
Option (E) yields the largest ratio, producing the largest orbit radius.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q4', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 4, false, 'A', 'Electric Field Symmetry & Superposition', 'medium', 'The picture shows 4 charges of equal magnitude fixed at the corners of a rectangle ($2\text{ cm} \times 4\text{ cm}$). How many locations on either Line A (vertical bisector) or Line B (horizontal bisector) would be places with zero net electric field?', '["1","2","All of Line A","All of Line B","All of both Line A and Line B"]'::jsonb, '(A) By symmetry, only at the exact geometric center of the rectangle do the electric field vectors from all four symmetrically placed identical charges cancel out vectorially to yield $\vec{E}_{\text{net}} = 0$. Moving away from the center along Line A or Line B introduces asymmetric distances to the pairs of charges, creating a nonzero net electric field component along the perpendicular axis. Thus, there is only $1$ point of zero net electric field.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q5', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 5, false, 'B', 'Resistivity & Conductor Geometry', 'easy', 'Four wires are each made of aluminum. Which wire will have the greatest electrical resistance?', '["Wire A (short, thick)","Wire B (longest, thinnest cross-section)","Wire C (short, thin)","Wire D (long, thick)","All the wires have the same resistance."]'::jsonb, '(B) The resistance of a cylindrical conductor of resistivity $\rho$, length $L$, and cross-sectional area $A$ is given by $R = \frac{\rho L}{A}$. Because all wires have the same resistivity $\rho$, resistance is maximized by choosing the maximum length $L$ and minimum cross-sectional area $A$ (Wire B).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q6', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 6, false, 'E', 'Magnetic Units & Dimensional Analysis', 'easy', 'Which of the following is NOT equal to one tesla?', '["$1\\text{ J}/(\\text{A}\\cdot\\text{m}^2)$","$1\\text{ kg}/(\\text{C}\\cdot\\text{s})$","$1\\text{ N}/(\\text{A}\\cdot\\text{m})$","$1\\text{ V}\\cdot\\text{s}/\\text{m}^2$","$1\\text{ A}\\cdot\\text{N}/\\text{V}$"]'::jsonb, '(E) From $F = qvB = ILB$, $1\text{ T} = 1\frac{\text{N}}{\text{A}\cdot\text{m}}$.
$\frac{\text{J}}{\text{A}\cdot\text{m}^2} = \frac{\text{N}\cdot\text{m}}{\text{A}\cdot\text{m}^2} = \frac{\text{N}}{\text{A}\cdot\text{m}} = 1\text{ T}$
$\frac{\text{kg}}{\text{C}\cdot\text{s}} = \frac{\text{kg}\cdot\text{m/s}^2}{(\text{C/s})\cdot\text{m}} = \frac{\text{N}}{\text{A}\cdot\text{m}} = 1\text{ T}$
$1\text{ V}\cdot\text{s}/\text{m}^2 = \frac{\text{J/C}\cdot\text{s}}{\text{m}^2} = \frac{\text{N}\cdot\text{m}}{\text{A}\cdot\text{m}^2} = 1\text{ T}$
Option (E): $1\frac{\text{A}\cdot\text{N}}{\text{V}} = \frac{\text{A}\cdot\text{N}}{\text{J/C}} = \frac{\text{A}^2\text{s}\cdot\text{N}}{\text{N}\cdot\text{m}} = \frac{\text{A}^2\text{s}}{\text{m}} \ne 1\text{ T}$.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q7', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 7, false, 'C', 'Gauss''s Law & Enclosed Electric Flux', 'easy', 'A cube with side length $d$ and a sphere with diameter $d$ each enclose the same net electric charge $+Q$. If $\Phi_C$ is the flux through the cube and $\Phi_S$ is the flux through the sphere, which of the following is true?', '["$\\Phi_C > \\Phi_S$","$\\Phi_C < \\Phi_S$","$\\Phi_C = \\Phi_S$","$\\Phi_C = 0$","$\\Phi_S = 0$"]'::jsonb, '(C) By Gauss''s Law, the total electric flux through any closed surface depends exclusively on the net enclosed charge: $\Phi_E = \oint \vec{E}\cdot d\vec{A} = \frac{Q_{\text{enc}}}{\varepsilon_0}$. Because both closed surfaces enclose identical net charge $+Q$, their total electric fluxes are equal: $\Phi_C = \Phi_S = \frac{Q}{\varepsilon_0}$.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q8', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 8, false, 'E', 'Electrostatic Potential Wells & Oscillations', 'medium', 'Two positive charges $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ of equal magnitude are fixed at $x = 0$ and $x = 100\text{ cm}$. A third positive charge $q$ is released from rest at $x = 70\text{ cm}$. At what position would it return to rest?', '["$x = 30\\text{ cm}$","$x = 50\\text{ cm}$","$x = 70\\text{ cm}$","Cannot be determined without $q$","It will not stop."]'::jsonb, '(E) In ideal electrostatic conditions without friction or radiation damping, energy is conserved. The symmetric potential energy well causes the charge to oscillate periodically between turning points $x = 70\text{ cm}$ and $x = 30\text{ cm}$, passing through $x = 50\text{ cm}$ at maximum speed. It continuously oscillates back and forth and does not come to a permanent stop.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q9', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 9, false, 'A', 'Elementary Charge Quantization', 'easy', 'An object carries a charge of $-1\text{ C}$. How many excess electrons does it contain?', '["$6.25 \\times 10^{18}$","$8.00 \\times 10^{18}$","$1.60 \\times 10^{19}$","$3.20 \\times 10^{19}$","$6.25 \\times 10^{19}$"]'::jsonb, '(A) Using the elementary charge $e = 1.6 \times 10^{-19}\text{ C}$:
  $$N = \frac{|Q|}{e} = \frac{1.0\text{ C}}{1.6 \times 10^{-19}\text{ C/electron}} = 6.25 \times 10^{18}\text{ electrons}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q10', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 10, false, 'E', 'DC Resistor Networks & Kirchhoff''s Rules', 'medium', 'Four $200\ \Omega$ resistors are configured such that $R_2, R_3, R_4$ are in parallel with each other, and that entire parallel block is in series with $R_1$ across a $24\text{ V}$ ideal battery. How much total current is provided by the source?', '["$30\\text{ mA}$","$48\\text{ mA}$","$64\\text{ mA}$","$72\\text{ mA}$","$90\\text{ mA}$"]'::jsonb, '(E) Equivalent resistance of three identical $200\ \Omega$ resistors in parallel:
  $$R_{234} = \frac{200\ \Omega}{3} \approx 66.67\ \Omega$$
Total series resistance:
  $$R_{\text{total}} = R_1 + R_{234} = 200 + \frac{200}{3} = \frac{800}{3}\ \Omega \approx 266.67\ \Omega$$
Total source current:
  $$I_{\text{total}} = \frac{V}{R_{\text{total}}} = \frac{24\text{ V}}{\frac{800}{3}\ \Omega} = \frac{72}{800}\text{ A} = 0.090\text{ A} = 90\text{ mA}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q11', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 11, false, 'E', 'Power Dissipation in Resistor Networks', 'medium', 'For the same circuit in Question 10, what is the ratio of the power dissipated by $R_1$ to the power dissipated by $R_4$?', '["$1/9$","$1/3$","$1$","$3$","$9$"]'::jsonb, '(E) The entire current $I$ passes through $R_1$, whereas in the parallel bank of three identical resistors, the current through $R_4$ is $I_4 = \frac{1}{3}I$. Since $R_1 = R_4 = R$, the ratio of power dissipation is:
  $$\frac{P_1}{P_4} = \frac{I^2 R_1}{I_4^2 R_4} = \frac{I^2 R}{\left(\frac{1}{3}I\right)^2 R} = \frac{I^2}{\frac{1}{9}I^2} = 9$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q12', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 12, false, 'D', 'RC Time Constant & Dimensional Analysis', 'easy', 'What is the value and unit of the product: $20\ \mu\text{F} \times 500\ \Omega$?', '["$0.01\\text{ henry}$","$0.01\\text{ ampere per coulomb}$","$0.01\\text{ weber}$","$0.01\\text{ second}$","$0.01\\text{ volt per ampere}$"]'::jsonb, '(D) Numerical value: $(20 \times 10^{-6}\text{ F}) \times (500\ \Omega) = 0.010$.
Dimensional analysis of the $RC$ time constant ($\tau = RC$):
  $$[\text{Farad}] \times [\text{Ohm}] = \left(\frac{\text{Coulomb}}{\text{Volt}}\right) \times \left(\frac{\text{Volt}}{\text{Ampere}}\right) = \frac{\text{C}}{\text{C/s}} = \text{second}$$
Thus, the result is $0.01\text{ second}$.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q13', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 13, false, 'B', 'Faraday''s Law & Induced Electric Fields', 'hard', 'A copper wire circle of radius $1\text{ m}$ is immersed in a magnetic field $B(t) = 2t(1-t) = 2t - 2t^2$ perpendicular into the page. What is the magnitude of the induced electric field in the wire at time $t = 1\text{ s}$?', '["$(1/\\pi)\\text{ N/C}$","$1\\text{ N/C}$","$2\\text{ N/C}$","$\\pi\\text{ N/C}$","$2\\pi\\text{ N/C}$"]'::jsonb, '(B) Faraday''s Law in non-electrostatic form relating $\vec{E}$ and magnetic flux $\Phi_B$:
  $$\oint \vec{E}\cdot d\vec{l} = -\frac{d\Phi_B}{dt} = -A\frac{dB}{dt}$$
  $$E(2\pi r) = -\pi r^2 \frac{d}{dt}(2t - 2t^2) = -\pi r^2 (2 - 4t)$$
  $$E = \frac{r}{2}|2 - 4t|$$
At $t = 1\text{ s}$ with $r = 1\text{ m}$:
  $$E = \frac{1}{2}|2 - 4(1)| = \frac{1}{2}(2) = 1\text{ N/C} \text{ (or V/m)}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q14', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 14, false, 'A', 'Magnetic Force on Current Loops & Equilibrium', 'medium', 'The top half of a rectangular loop of wire ($x\text{ m} \times y\text{ m}$) hangs vertically in a uniform magnetic field $B$ pointing into the page, supporting a mass $m$. Find the magnitude and direction of the loop current to balance the mass''s weight.', '["$I = \\frac{mg}{xB}$, clockwise","$I = \\frac{mg}{xB}$, counterclockwise","$I = \\frac{mg}{(x + \\frac{1}{2}y)B}$, clockwise","$I = \\frac{mg}{(x + \\frac{1}{2}y)B}$, counterclockwise","$I = \\frac{mg}{(x + y)B}$, clockwise"]'::jsonb, '(A) The magnetic forces on the vertical segments of length $y/2$ are equal and opposite, canceling each other out. The top horizontal wire of length $x$ must produce an upward magnetic force balancing gravity:
  $$F_B = I x B = mg \implies I = \frac{mg}{xB}$$
By the right-hand rule ($\vec{F}_B = I\vec{L}\times\vec{B}$), with $\vec{B}$ directed into the page, an upward force requires current pointing to the right along the top wire, which corresponds to a clockwise circulation.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q15', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 15, false, 'C', 'Electrostatic Potential Energy & Energy Conservation', 'medium', 'Particle $q_1 = 10\text{ nC}$ is fixed in place. Particle $q_2 = -5\text{ nC}$ of mass $m = 5 \times 10^{-10}\text{ kg}$ is released from rest $2\text{ cm}$ away from $q_1$. How fast will it move when it is $1\text{ cm}$ away?', '["$100\\text{ m/s}$","$200\\text{ m/s}$","$300\\text{ m/s}$","$400\\text{ m/s}$","$500\\text{ m/s}$"]'::jsonb, '(C) Change in electric potential from $r_i = 0.02\text{ m}$ to $r_f = 0.01\text{ m}$:
  $$\Delta V = k q_1 \left(\frac{1}{r_f} - \frac{1}{r_i}\right) = (9 \times 10^9)(10 \times 10^{-9})\left(\frac{1}{0.01} - \frac{1}{0.02}\right) = 90(100 - 50) = +4500\text{ V}$$
Change in electric potential energy:
  $$\Delta U_E = q_2 \Delta V = (-5 \times 10^{-9}\text{ C})(4500\text{ V}) = -2.25 \times 10^{-5}\text{ J}$$
By Conservation of Energy ($\Delta K = -\Delta U_E$):
  $$\frac{1}{2}m v_f^2 = 2.25 \times 10^{-5}\text{ J} \implies v_f = \sqrt{\frac{2(2.25 \times 10^{-5}\text{ J})}{5 \times 10^{-10}\text{ kg}}} = \sqrt{9 \times 10^4} = 300\text{ m/s}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q16', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 16, false, 'A', 'Magnetic Fields of Parallel Current Wires', 'medium', 'Two long straight parallel wires carry current: Wire 1 carries $I$ to the right, and Wire 2 carries $2I$ to the left. At which of the marked points is the net magnetic field zero?', '["Point 1 only (above Wire 1)","Points 1 and 2 only","Point 2 only","Points 3 and 4 only","Point 3 only"]'::jsonb, '(A) Between the wires, right-hand rule shows both currents produce magnetic fields pointing into the page, so they add constructively and cannot cancel.
Above Wire 1 (Point 1), Wire 1 produces a field pointing out of the page ($B_1 = \frac{\mu_0 I}{2\pi d}$), while Wire 2 produces a field pointing into the page ($B_2 = \frac{\mu_0 (2I)}{2\pi (2d)} = \frac{\mu_0 I}{2\pi d}$). Because the fields are equal in magnitude and opposite in direction, the net magnetic field at Point 1 is zero.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q17', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 17, false, 'A', 'Coulomb''s Law & Variable Acceleration', 'easy', 'A charge $+q$ is released from rest near a fixed charge $+Q$. Which graph best depicts the acceleration $a$ of $+q$ as a function of its distance $r$ from $+Q$?', '["Decreasing curve proportional to $1/r^2$","Decreasing linear line","Increasing curve","Horizontal line (constant)","Exponential increase"]'::jsonb, '(A) By Coulomb''s Law and Newton''s Second Law:
  $$a(r) = \frac{F_E}{m} = \frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\frac{Qq}{m r^2} \propto \frac{1}{r^2}$$
The acceleration decreases monotonically following an inverse-square relationship with distance $r$, which is correctly illustrated in graph (A).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q18', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 18, false, 'C', 'Parallel Capacitors & Charge Redistribution', 'medium', 'Capacitors $C_1 = 3\ \mu\text{F}$ (initially $Q_1 = 24\ \mu\text{C}$) and $C_2 = 6\ \mu\text{F}$ (initially $Q_2 = 12\ \mu\text{C}$) are connected in parallel when switch S is closed. How much charge will be stored on the positive plate of the $6\ \mu\text{F}$ capacitor at equilibrium?', '["$18\\ \\mu\\text{C}$","$20\\ \\mu\\text{C}$","$24\\ \\mu\\text{C}$","$27\\ \\mu\\text{C}$","$36\\ \\mu\\text{C}$"]'::jsonb, '(C) Total initial charge on the plates:
  $$Q_{\text{total}} = 24\ \mu\text{C} + 12\ \mu\text{C} = 36\ \mu\text{C}$$
In parallel, both capacitors share the same potential difference $V = \frac{Q_{\text{total}}}{C_1 + C_2} = \frac{36\ \mu\text{C}}{3\ \mu\text{F} + 6\ \mu\text{F}} = 4\text{ V}$.
Final charge on the $6\ \mu\text{F}$ capacitor ($C_2$):
  $$Q_2'' = C_2 V = (6\ \mu\text{F})(4\text{ V}) = 24\ \mu\text{C}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q19', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 19, false, 'E', 'Motional EMF & Cross Product Geometry', 'medium', 'A metal bar of length $L$ is pulled with velocity $v$ through a uniform magnetic field $B$ pointing into the plane of the page. The velocity $v$ is parallel to the magnetic field. What is the voltage produced between the ends of the bar?', '["$vB$, with point X at a higher potential than point Y","$vB$, with point Y at a higher potential than point X","$vBL$, with point X at a higher potential than point Y","$vBL$, with point Y at a higher potential than point X","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '(E) The magnetic force on mobile conduction electrons within the conductor is given by $\vec{F}_B = q(\vec{v} \times \vec{B})$. Because the velocity vector $\vec{v}$ is parallel to the magnetic field vector $\vec{B}$, the cross product is zero ($\vec{v} \times \vec{B} = 0$). No charge separation occurs along the bar, producing zero motional emf ($\mathcal{E} = 0\text{ V}$). Therefore, (E) is the correct choice.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q20', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 20, false, 'E', 'Electric Dipole Field at Midpoint', 'medium', 'An electric dipole consists of a pair of equal but opposite point charges of magnitude $4.0\text{ nC}$ separated by a distance of $2.0\text{ cm}$. What is the electric field strength at the point midway between the charges?', '["$9.0 \\times 10^4\\text{ V/m}$","$1.8 \\times 10^5\\text{ V/m}$","$3.6 \\times 10^5\\text{ V/m}$","$7.2 \\times 10^4\\text{ V/m}$","$7.2 \\times 10^5\\text{ V/m}$"]'::jsonb, '(E) The midpoint is at distance $r = 1.0\text{ cm} = 0.01\text{ m}$ from each charge. Electric field lines point away from $+q$ and toward $-q$, so the electric field vectors from both charges point in the exact same direction and add constructively:
  $$E_{\text{net}} = E_+ + E_- = 2\left(\frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\frac{q}{r^2}\right) = 2(9.0 \times 10^9)\frac{4.0 \times 10^{-9}\text{ C}}{(0.01\text{ m})^2} = 18.0 \times \frac{4.0}{1.0 \times 10^{-4}} = 7.2 \times 10^5\text{ V/m}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q21', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 21, false, 'E', 'Spherical Capacitor Capacitance Derivation', 'hard', 'Two concentric spherical metal shells have radii $R$ and $2R$, respectively. Find the capacitance of this spherical capacitor.', '["$\\frac{1}{8\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R}$","$\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R}$","$2\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R$","$4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R$","$8\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R$"]'::jsonb, '(E) The potential difference between two concentric spherical conductors of radii $a = R$ and $b = 2R$ carrying charge $+Q$ and $-Q$ is:
  $$\Delta V = \frac{Q}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\left(\frac{1}{R} - \frac{1}{2R}\right) = \frac{Q}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\left(\frac{1}{2R}\right) = \frac{Q}{8\pi\varepsilon_0 R}$$
The capacitance is defined by $C = \frac{Q}{\Delta V}$:
  $$C = \frac{Q}{\frac{Q}{8\pi\varepsilon_0 R}} = 8\pi\varepsilon_0 R$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q22', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 22, false, 'C', 'Magnetic Force Work & Constant Speed', 'easy', 'Traveling at an initial speed of $1.5 \times 10^6\text{ m/s}$, a proton enters a region of constant magnetic field $B = 1.0\text{ T}$ at an angle of $30^\circ$ with $\vec{B}$. Compute the proton''s speed $4\text{ s}$ after entering the magnetic field.', '["$5.0 \\times 10^5\\text{ m/s}$","$7.5 \\times 10^5\\text{ m/s}$","$1.5 \\times 10^6\\text{ m/s}$","$3.0 \\times 10^6\\text{ m/s}$","$6.0 \\times 10^6\\text{ m/s}$"]'::jsonb, '(C) The magnetic force $\vec{F}_B = q(\vec{v} \times \vec{B})$ is strictly perpendicular to the velocity vector $\vec{v}$ at all times. Consequently, the magnetic work done on the charged particle is zero ($W = \int \vec{F}_B \cdot d\vec{r} = 0$), meaning the kinetic energy and orbital speed remain constant over time. Thus, the speed remains $1.5 \times 10^6\text{ m/s}$.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q23', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 23, false, 'B', 'LR Circuit Initial Response ($t = 0^+$)', 'medium', 'An $LR$ circuit consists of a battery $\mathcal{E}$, resistor $r$, and a parallel branch with resistor $R$ and inductor $L$ in series with another resistor $R$. What is the current through $r$ immediately after switch S is closed?', '["$0$","$\\frac{\\mathcal{E}}{r + R}$","$\\frac{\\mathcal{E}}{r + 2R}$","$\\frac{\\mathcal{E}}{2r + R}$","$\\frac{2\\mathcal{E}}{2r + R}$"]'::jsonb, '(B) At $t = 0^+$, the inductor produces an infinite back-emf opposing any instantaneous change in current, acting as an open circuit ($I_L(0^+) = 0$). Therefore, current only flows through the loop consisting of resistor $r$ and the single parallel resistor $R$. The initial current is:
  $$I(0^+) = \frac{\mathcal{E}}{r + R}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q24', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 24, false, 'B', 'LR Circuit Steady-State Inductor Energy', 'hard', 'After the switch has been kept closed for a long time in the circuit of Question 23, how much energy is stored in the inductor?', '["$\\frac{L\\mathcal{E}^2}{(2r+R)^2}$","$\\frac{L\\mathcal{E}^2}{2(2r+R)^2}$","$\\frac{L\\mathcal{E}^2}{4(2r+R)^2}$","$\\frac{RL\\mathcal{E}^2}{8(2r+R)^2}$","$\\frac{L\\mathcal{E}^2}{8(2r+R)^2}$"]'::jsonb, '(B) As $t \to \infty$, the inductor acts as a short circuit with zero resistance. The parallel combination of the two branches (each containing resistance $R$) is $R_{\text{parallel}} = R/2$.
Total equivalent resistance is $R_{\text{eq}} = r + R/2 = \frac{2r+R}{2}$.
Total current from the source: $I_{\text{total}} = \frac{\mathcal{E}}{r + R/2} = \frac{2\mathcal{E}}{2r + R}$.
Because both parallel branches have equal resistance $R$, the current splits evenly, so the inductor branch current is $I_L = \frac{1}{2}I_{\text{total}} = \frac{\mathcal{E}}{2r + R}$.
The magnetic energy stored is:
  $$U_L = \frac{1}{2}L I_L^2 = \frac{1}{2}L\left(\frac{\mathcal{E}}{2r+R}\right)^2 = \frac{L\mathcal{E}^2}{2(2r+R)^2}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q25', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 25, false, 'A', 'LR Circuit Switch Opening Dynamics', 'medium', 'After having been closed for a long time, the switch S is suddenly opened. What is the current through $r$ immediately after S is opened?', '["$0$","$\\frac{\\mathcal{E}}{r+R}$","$\\frac{\\mathcal{E}}{r+2R}$","$\\frac{\\mathcal{E}}{2r+R}$","$\\frac{2\\mathcal{E}}{2r+R}$"]'::jsonb, '(A) Opening the switch mechanically isolates the branch containing resistor $r$ from the closed loop. The current through $r$ drops instantaneously to zero ($I_r = 0$). The inductor continues to drive current through the remaining closed loop containing the two $R$ resistors until its stored magnetic energy is dissipated.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q26', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 26, false, 'C', 'Electrostatic Shielding & Cavity Fields', 'hard', 'A solid neutral metal sphere of radius $6a$ contains an off-center spherical cavity of radius $a$ enclosing a point charge $+q$. If $E_X$ and $E_Y$ denote the electric field strengths at exterior points X and Y located at the same distance $r > 6a$ from the center of the sphere, which of the following is true?', '["$E_X = 16E_Y$","$E_X = \\left(\\frac{11}{5}\\right)E_Y$","$E_X = E_Y$","$E_X = \\left(\\frac{11}{5}\\right)^2 E_Y$","$E_X = 0$"]'::jsonb, '(C) By electrostatic shielding, charge $-q$ distributes on the inner cavity wall, leaving $+q$ distributed uniformly over the outer spherical conductor surface. Outside the conducting sphere ($r > 6a$), the external electric field is identical to that of a point charge $+q$ located at the sphere''s geometric center:
  $$E(r) = \frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\frac{q}{r^2}$$
Because points X and Y are equidistant from the center of the sphere, $E_X = E_Y$.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q27', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 27, false, 'B', 'Electric Potential & Work by Electric Field', 'medium', 'Two charges $+Q$ are fixed at $(0, 4\text{ m})$ and $(0, -4\text{ m})$. Determine the work done by the electric field to move a charge $-Q$ from infinity to point P at $(3\text{ m}, 0)$.', '["$\\frac{2kQ^2}{3}$","$\\frac{2kQ^2}{5}$","$-\\frac{2kQ^2}{5}$","$\\frac{kQ^2}{5}$","$-\\frac{3kQ^2}{5}$"]'::jsonb, '(B) The distance from each $+Q$ charge to point P is $r = \sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = 5\text{ m}$.
The electric potential at point P is:
  $$V_P = \frac{kQ}{5} + \frac{kQ}{5} = \frac{2kQ}{5}$$
The work done by the electric field is $W_E = -\Delta U = -q(V_P - V_\infty)$:
  $$W_E = -(-Q)\left(\frac{2kQ}{5} - 0\right) = +\frac{2kQ^2}{5}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q28', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 28, false, 'E', 'RL Circuit Exponential Rise Time Constant', 'medium', 'A circuit connects a battery $\mathcal{E}$, switch, resistor $R$, and inductor $L$ in series. How long will it take for the current to reach $99\%$ of its maximum value?', '["$(\\ln 0.99)\\frac{L}{R}$","$(\\ln 99)RL$","$\\left(\\ln\\frac{1}{100}\\right)\\frac{L}{R}$","$\\left(\\ln\\frac{100}{99}\\right)\\frac{L}{R}$","$(\\ln 100)\\frac{L}{R}$"]'::jsonb, '(E) The current in a charging $RL$ circuit obeys $I(t) = I_{\max}(1 - e^{-t/\tau})$, where $\tau = L/R$. Setting $I(t) = 0.99 I_{\max}$:
  $$1 - e^{-t/\tau} = 0.99 \implies e^{-t/\tau} = 0.01 = \frac{1}{100}$$
  $$-\frac{t}{\tau} = \ln\left(\frac{1}{100}\right) = -\ln(100) \implies t = (\ln 100)\tau = (\ln 100)\frac{L}{R}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q29', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 29, false, 'E', 'Parallel Appliance Circuits & Fuse Limits', 'easy', 'What is the maximum number of $40\text{ W}$ light bulbs that can be connected in parallel across a $120\text{ V}$ source without exceeding a $5\text{ A}$ fuse limit?', '["6","9","12","14","15"]'::jsonb, '(E) The maximum total electric power available without blowing the fuse is:
  $$P_{\max} = V I_{\max} = (120\text{ V})(5\text{ A}) = 600\text{ W}$$
The number of $40\text{ W}$ bulbs in parallel is:
  $$N = \frac{P_{\max}}{P_{\text{bulb}}} = \frac{600\text{ W}}{40\text{ W}} = 15 \text{ bulbs}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q30', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 30, false, 'D', 'Faraday''s Law, Lenz''s Law & Induced Current', 'easy', 'A loop of wire is situated in a magnetic field $\vec{B}$ pointing out of the page. If $B$ decreases uniformly in strength, the induced current in the loop is:', '["clockwise and decreasing","clockwise and increasing","counterclockwise and decreasing","counterclockwise and constant","counterclockwise and increasing"]'::jsonb, '(D) As the out-of-page flux decreases, by Lenz''s Law the induced current must create out-of-page magnetic flux to oppose this change, which requires a counterclockwise current. Because $B(t)$ decreases uniformly ($\frac{dB}{dt} = \text{constant}$), the induced electromotive force $\mathcal{E} = -A\frac{dB}{dt}$ and the resulting induced current $I = \mathcal{E}/R$ are nonzero and constant.')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

  $$C_{\text{vac}} = \frac{\varepsilon_0 A}{d/3} = \frac{3\varepsilon_0 A}{d}, \quad C_{\text{diel}} = \frac{K\varepsilon_0 A}{d/3} = \frac{3K\varepsilon_0 A}{d}$$
  $$\frac{1}{C_{\text{eq}}} = \frac{1}{C_{\text{vac}}} + \frac{1}{C_{\text{diel}}} + \frac{1}{C_{\text{vac}}} = \frac{2d}{3\varepsilon_0 A} + \frac{d}{3K\varepsilon_0 A} = \frac{d(2K+1)}{3K\varepsilon_0 A}$$
  $$C_{\text{eq}} = \frac{3K\varepsilon_0 A}{d(2K+1)}$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q32', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 32, false, 'A', 'Dipole Electric Field vs. Potential Zeros', 'medium', 'For two charges $+Q$ and $-Q$ separated by a finite distance, at how many points in the plane of the page are both the electric field and the electric potential equal to zero?', '["0","1","2","Infinite","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '(A) The electric potential $V = 0$ along the perpendicular bisector line. However, on this line, the electric fields from the two charges add constructively parallel to the dipole axis, meaning $\vec{E} \ne 0$ everywhere in the finite plane. Thus, there are $0$ points where both $\vec{E} = 0$ and $V = 0$ simultaneously (except at infinity).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q33', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 33, false, 'D', 'Ampere''s Law Line Integrals', 'medium', 'Four wires pass perpendicularly through a square $WXYZ$, carrying currents $I, 2I, 3I, 4I$ with orientations as labeled (two in, two out). What is the absolute value of $\oint \vec{B}\cdot d\vec{\ell}$ around $WXYZ$?', '["$\\frac{1}{2}\\mu_0 I$","$\\mu_0 I$","$\\sqrt{5}\\mu_0 I$","$2\\mu_0 I$","$5\\mu_0 I$"]'::jsonb, '(D) By Ampere''s Law: $\oint \vec{B}\cdot d\vec{\ell} = \mu_0 I_{\text{enc}}$.
The net enclosed current is the algebraic sum taking directions into account:
  $$I_{\text{enc}} = (2I + 4I) - (I + 3I) = 6I - 4I = 2I$$
  $$\left|\oint \vec{B}\cdot d\vec{\ell}\right| = 2\mu_0 I$$'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q34', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 34, false, 'B', 'Conservative Electric Fields & Path Independence', 'easy', 'A charge $+q$ is moved from point A to point B along Path 1. What would the change in potential energy be if the charge moved along Path 2 from point A to point B instead?', '["Double","The same","Negative of the original","Zero","Cannot be determined"]'::jsonb, '(B) The electrostatic force is conservative, which means the electrostatic potential difference $\Delta V = V_B - V_A$ and the corresponding change in potential energy $\Delta U = q\Delta V$ depend entirely on the initial and final endpoints and are completely independent of the path taken.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-1_q35', 'ap-mock-physc-em-1', 35, false, 'A', '2D Electrostatic Vector Forces & Acceleration', 'hard', 'Two point charges $+Q$ are fixed a distance $L$ apart. A particle of charge $-q$ and mass $m$ is placed symmetrically on the perpendicular bisector at distance $L/2$. What is its initial acceleration when released from rest?', '["$\\frac{\\sqrt{2}Qq}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0 L^2 m}$","$\\frac{Qq}{\\pi\\varepsilon_0 L^2 m}$","$\\frac{2Qq}{\\pi\\varepsilon_0 L^2 m}$","$\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}Qq}{\\pi\\varepsilon_0 L^2 m}$","$\\frac{4Qq}{\\pi\\varepsilon_0 L^2 m}$"]'::jsonb, '(A) The distance from $-q$ to each $+Q$ is $r = \sqrt{(L/2)^2 + (L/2)^2} = \frac{L}{\sqrt{2}}$.
The force from each $+Q$ charge has magnitude:
  $$F_1 = \frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\frac{Qq}{r^2} = \frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\frac{Qq}{L^2/2} = \frac{2Qq}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 L^2}$$
The horizontal components cancel, and the vertical components add at an angle of $45^\circ$:
  $$F_{\text{net}} = 2 F_1 \cos 45^\circ = 2\left(\frac{2Qq}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 L^2}\right)\left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right) = \frac{\sqrt{2}Qq}{2\pi\varepsilon_0 L^2}$$
Acceleration $a = \frac{F_{\text{net}}}{m} = \frac{\sqrt{2}Qq}{2\pi\varepsilon_0 L^2 m}$.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q1', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 1, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'In an RC discharging circuit, a capacitor C with initial voltage V0 discharges through a resistor R when a switch is closed at t = 0. Which of the following functions represents the voltage V(t) across the capacitor as a function of time?', '["V(t) = V0 (1 - e^{-t / (RC)})","V(t) = V0 t / (RC)","V(t) = V0 (1 - t / (RC))","V(t) = V0 e^{-t / (RC)}","V(t) = V0 e^{-t / (2RC)}"]'::jsonb, 'During capacitor discharge through resistance R, Kirchhoff''s loop rule gives -R (dq/dt) - q/C = 0 ==> dq/dt = -q / (RC). The solution is q(t) = Q0 e^{-t / (RC)}. Dividing by capacitance C yields the capacitor voltage V(t) = q(t)/C = V0 e^{-t / (RC)}, which decays exponentially to zero.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q2', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 2, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'A loop of wire of radius r and resistance R is exposed to a magnetic field pointing into the page that steadily increases from zero to magnitude B in time t, as shown. Describe the induced current.', '["\\pirB/t, clockwise","\\pirB/t, counterclockwise","\\pir2B/Rt, clockwise","\\pir2B/Rt, counterclockwise","\\pir2B/t, counterclockwise"]'::jsonb, '(D) First, to oppose the increase in inward flux, the induced current must flow counterclockwise, producing an outward flux. What is the magnitude of the current? The induced EMF is Because the magnetic field increases linearly, its slope dB/dt is simply The resistance is R, so according to Ohm’s law the current is I = V/R = AB/Rt. Finally, to express the result in terms of the variables in the problem, A = \pir2, so I = \pir2 B/Rt.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q3', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 3, false, 'B', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'The potential in a region of space is given by the formula V = ar, where r is a radial coordinate and a is a positive constant. Which of the following statements is true of this region?', '["The electric field has magnitude a and points away from the origin of the coordinates .","The electric field has a magnitude a and points toward the origin .","The electric field has a magnitude r2/2a and points away from the origin .","The electric field has a magnitude r2/2a and points toward the origin .","The electric force is nonconservative."]'::jsonb, '(B) The electric field equals the negative derivative of the potential along any line, and this holds for a line passing through the radius. Mathematically, Er = −dV/dr = −a. Even without recalling this equation in its entirety, one could arrive at the correct answer by combining the information that (1) the magnitude of the electric field is related to the derivative of the potential, and (2) the electric field always points in the direction of decreasing potential. Choice (E) is false. Any field that has an associated potential function is conservative. (That is, any field for which a potential energy function can be defined is a conservative field.)'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q4', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 4, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'A capacitor of capacitance 25 \muF is charged to 300 V and then connected in series with an inductor of inductance 10 mH. The maximum current flowing through the circuit will be', '["7,500 A","500 A","300 A","15 A","2.5 A"]'::jsonb, '(D) The loop rule for an LC circuit is . This looks just like a harmonic oscillator with . The current is given by and the maximum current obeys Imax = \omegaQmax. The maximum charge is the initial charge of the capacitor, .'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q5', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 5, false, 'A', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'If it requires an input of E joules to move a charge Q from point A to point B at constant velocity, which of the following is true?', '["VB − VA = E/Q","VB − VA = −E/Q","VB − VA = QE","VB − VA = −QE","VB − VA = Q/E"]'::jsonb, '(A) Potential is equal to the potential energy per unit charge, so only choices (A) and (B) have the correct units. To figure out the signs, consider the case where Q and E are positive. If positive energy is required to move the charge from point A to point B, point B must be at higher potential energy (uphill), so Q(VB − VA) must be positive. Because E is positive, only the equation VB − VA = E/Q has signs that are consistent with this situation.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q6', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 6, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'A parallel-plate vacuum capacitor with capacitance C0 is connected to a battery of voltage V. While still connected to the battery, a dielectric slab with dielectric constant kappa = 3 is inserted to fill the space between the plates. What is the ratio of the new stored energy to the initial stored energy (U_new / U_initial)?', '["1/9","1/3","1","3","9"]'::jsonb, 'Because the capacitor remains connected to the battery, the voltage V across the plates stays constant. The capacitance increases to C_new = kappa C0 = 3 C0. The energy stored in a capacitor at fixed voltage is U = 1/2 C V^2. Therefore, U_new / U_initial = (1/2 (3 C0) V^2) / (1/2 C0 V^2) = 3.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q7', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 7, false, 'E', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'A battery with EMF of V and an internal resistance of rint is connected to a heating loop with resistance R. What is the correct expression for the rate at which energy is converted to heat by the heating loop?', '["V2/R","V2rint/(rint + R)2","V/(rint + R)","V2R2/(rint + R)2","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '(E) The current through the circuit is The power dissipated through the heating element is then Because this is not equivalent to any of the given choices, the answer is choice (E).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q8', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 8, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'A parallel plate capacitor connected to a battery contains a plastic dielectric material between the plates of the capacitor. Removing the dielectric has what effect on the electric field between the plates and the charge on the plates?', '["Electric field and charge both decrease.","Electric field and charge both increase.","Electric field increases and the charge remains the same.","Electric field remains the same and the charge decreases.","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '(D) The equation always holds, so V = Ed is valid even in the case of a dielectric. Therefore, because the capacitor is always connected to the battery and the plate separation distance is unchanged, V and d are fixed and so E must remain the same. What effect does this have on the plate charge? The key equation here is Q = CV. The capacitance decreases in the absence of the dielectric. The voltage is held fixed (because the capacitor is attached to the battery), so the charge on the plates must decrease. How is it that the electric field can remain constant while the charge on the plates decreases? In the presence of the dielectric, the charge on the plates is partially screened by the induced charge on the surface of the dielectric. Once the dielectric is removed, the charge on the plates no longer needs to cancel this induced charge, so it decreases.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q9', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 9, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'An electron (charge -e, mass m) is released from rest at distance r1 from a fixed proton (charge +e). What is the kinetic energy of the electron when it has accelerated to a distance r2 from the proton (where r2 < r1)?', '["k e^2 * (1/r1 - 1/r2)","Zero","k e^2 / (2 r2)","k e^2 * (1/r2 - 1/r1)","1/2 m (k e^2 / r2)^2"]'::jsonb, 'Mechanical energy is conserved: K_initial + U_initial = K_final + U_final. The electron starts from rest, so K_initial = 0. The electrostatic potential energy is U(r) = -k e^2 / r. Therefore, 0 - k e^2 / r1 = K_final - k e^2 / r2 ==> K_final = k e^2 (1/r2 - 1/r1). Because r2 < r1, 1/r2 > 1/r1, so the kinetic energy is positive.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q10', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 10, false, 'C', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'The magnetic field created by the two wires shown at point A in the figure is B0 (pointing out of the page), and the two wires carry identical currents in the same direction. Which of the following is true?', '["Both wires carry a current of 4\\pid/3\\mu0 toward the top of the page.","Both wires carry a current of 4\\pid/3\\mu0 toward the bottom of the page.","Both wires carry a current of 4\\pidB0/\\mu0 toward the top of the page.","Both wires carry a current of 4\\pidB0/\\mu0 toward the bottom of the page.","It is impossible for the wires to produce a magnetic field pointing perpendicular to the page."]'::jsonb, '(C) A little experimentation with the right-hand rule shows that for points in between two wires carrying current in the same direction, the two wires produce magnetic fields in opposite directions. The wire closer to the point produces a larger magnetic field, so the net magnetic field points in the direction determined by this wire. In this case, because the magnetic field points out of the page, the current through the wire closest to point A (the right wire) must move toward the top of the page. The magnitude of the magnetic field due to a current-carrying wire is B = \mu0I/2\pir. Because the two wires produce fields in opposite directions, in this case the net magnetic field has magnitude Solving this equation for the current yields I = 4\pidB0/\mu0, so the correct answer is choice (C).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q11', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 11, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'Two insulating spheres of equal radii are oppositely charged with uniform charge densities of equal magnitude, as shown in the top half of the figure. The spheres are placed far apart from each other and can be considered completely isolated from each other. What is the effect of exchanging the top halves of these distributions with one another, as shown in the bottom half of the figure?', '["The potential increases at point A and decreases at point B.","The electric field magnitude increases at point A and decreases at point B.","The potential increases at both points A and B.","The electric field magnitude increases at both points A and B.","After the exchange, positive work is required to move a positive charge from point A to point B."]'::jsonb, '(D) Recall that potential is given by the integral . Therefore, moving negative charge closer to point A and positive charge farther away from point A decreases the potential at point A. The opposite occurs at point B, causing the potential to increase at point B. Therefore, choices (A) and (C) are false. Because of symmetry, a sphere of uniform charge density produces a zero net field at its center. Therefore, the electric field at points A and B is initially zero. Exchanging the tops of the spheres leads to nonzero electric fields at points A and B (pointing up at point A and down at point B). Therefore, (B) is false, while (D) is true. As a result of the symmetry of the situation, points A and B have the same potential. Therefore, zero work is required to move any charge from point A to point B, making choice (E) false.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q12', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 12, false, 'B', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'A loop of wire is pushed with constant velocity through a region of uniform magnetic field as shown. (The field points out of the page.) As the loop enters and exits the magnetic field, only one of the vertical segments of wire is within the magnetic field. What is the direction of the induced current within this vertical segment?', '["upward when both entering and exiting the magnetic field","downward when both entering and exiting the magnetic field","upward when entering the magnetic field and downward when exiting the magnetic field","downward when entering the magnetic field and upward when exiting the magnetic field","zero, because the segment of wire is perpendicular to the magnetic field"]'::jsonb, '(B) While entering the magnetic field, the induced current flows clockwise, moving downward through the right segment of the loop that is exposed to the magnetic field. While leaving the magnetic field, the induced current flows counterclockwise, moving downward through the left segment of the loop that is exposed to the magnetic field.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q13', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 13, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'What is the force on a positive charge +Q located on a 10 V equipotential line?', '["10 Q in magnitude, pointing parallel to the line","10 Q in magnitude, pointing perpendicular to the line in the direction of decreasing potential","10 Q in magnitude, pointing perpendicular to the line in the direction of increasing potential","of unknown magnitude, pointing perpendicular to the line in the direction of decreasing potential","of unknown magnitude, pointing perpendicular to the line in the direction of increasing potential"]'::jsonb, '(D) The force on the charge is proportional to the electric field according to the equation F = qE. The electric field is the negative derivative of the potential, but a single value of potential alone does not indicate anything about the electric field or the electric force. However, we can still determine the direction of the force. Electric fields are always perpendicular to equipotential curves, pointing in the direction of decreasing potential. Because the charge is positive, the force is parallel to the electric field.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q14', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 14, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'The capacitor shown consists of two concentric metallic cylindrical shells. Assuming that the radius of the inner cylinder, r, is much greater than the plate separation distance, d, what is the approximate capacitance of the capacitor?', '["C = \\epsilon0\\pir2/d","C = \\epsilon0\\pirl/d","C = \\epsilon0\\pidl/r","C = 2\\epsilon0\\pirl/d","C = \\epsilon0d/2\\pirl"]'::jsonb, '(D) Because the plate separation d is very small compared to the radius of the cylinder, it is reasonable to approximate the capacitor as a parallel plate capacitor with plate separation d and plate area 2\pirl. (This makes sense: Because d << r, the electric field is relatively constant between the plates and is perpendicular to the plates.) Therefore, the approximate capacitance is Even without making this observation, a few choices can be eliminated. Compared to the formula C = \epsilon0A/d, choice (E) has incorrect units, so it can be eliminated. Choice (A) does not include the length of the capacitor. However, we know from the simpler case of a parallel plate capacitor that the dimension of the plates is relevant to the value of the capacitance, so we can safely eliminate choice (A).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q15', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 15, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'A sphere has a uniform charge density distributed throughout its interior. What effect does doubling the radius of the sphere (while maintaining the same charge density) have on the electric field at a point outside the enlarged sphere?', '["The field remains the same.","The field increases by a factor of 2.","The field increases by a factor of 4.","The field increases by a factor of 8.","The field increases by a factor of 16."]'::jsonb, '(D) Imagine a spherical gaussian surface passing through any point outside the enlarged sphere. Doubling the radius of the sphere increases the enclosed charge by a factor of 8 (because the volume of a sphere is proportional to r3 and the charge is proportional to the volume). Thus, by Gauss’s law, the flux through the gaussian surface (whose surface area is fixed) increases by a factor of 8, and thus so does the electric field.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q16', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 16, false, 'A', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'A charged particle moves in a planar, circular orbit as a result of the presence of a uniform magnetic field perpendicular to the plane of its motion. Holding all other variables (such as the magnetic field, the particle’s velocity, and the particle’s charge) constant, which graph shows the relationship between the particle’s mass and the radius of its path?', '["Directly proportional to the parameter (linear scaling)","Inversely proportional to the square of the separation","Equal in magnitude with opposite orientation","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(A) The magnetic force provides the centripetal force: mv2/r = qvB. Rearranging this equation to solve for r in terms of m yields r = (v/qB)m. Therefore, the graph is linear, passing through the origin.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q17', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 17, false, 'A', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'A thin uniformly charged rod with negative linear charge density -lambda lies along the y-axis from y = -a to y = +a. What is the direction of the electric field produced by the rod at a point P on the positive x-axis at (x, 0)?', '["Directly toward the origin (in the -x direction)","Directly away from the origin (in the +x direction)","In the +y direction","In the -y direction","The electric field is zero"]'::jsonb, 'Consider symmetric elements of charge dq = -lambda dy at +y and -y on the rod. Because the charge is negative, the electric field vectors dE point toward the charge elements. By symmetry, their y-components cancel out, while their x-components add constructively pointing along the -x direction (toward the origin). Thus the net electric field points directly toward the origin in the -x direction.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q18', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 18, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'Consider a circuit consisting of the following elements in a single loop: a battery, a resistor, a switch, and a fourth unknown element E. A spark is most likely to occur at the switch if', '["E is a capacitor and the switch is being closed for the first time.","E is a capacitor and the switch is being opened after the circuit has reached equilibrium.","E is an inductor and the switch is being closed for the first time.","E is an inductor and the switch is being opened after the circuit has reached equilibrium.","E is a battery arranged in series with the other battery in the circuit."]'::jsonb, '(D) Recall that the current flowing through inductors is generally continuous because a discontinuous current would cause the time derivative of the current to be infinite and thus produce an infinite voltage. How is this theory applicable to the current problem? In choice (D), when the switch is opened, an abrupt change in the current occurs that produces a very large induced EMF, causing a spark to instantaneously connect the recently opened terminals of the switch. [In choice (C), the current is zero before and immediately after closing the switch, so there is no discontinuity and no spark.]'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q19', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 19, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'The two charge distributions shown each contain a net charge +Q uniformly distributed along an arc of a circle with radius r. In distribution A this charge is spread along an arc twice the length of the arc in distribution B. Which of the following comparisons of the potential V and the magnitude E of the electric field at point A and point B are true?', '["VA > VB and EA > EB","VA > VB and EA < EB","VA = VB and EA > EB","VA = VB and EA < EB","VA < VB and EA > EB"]'::jsonb, '(D) All the charge in both distributions is at a fixed distance r from the points of interest. Therefore, the potential at both point A and point B is V = Q/4\pi\epsilon0r and these potientials are equal. The electric field sums as vectors rather than scalars, so it is more complicated. In both cases, the net electric field points in the +x- direction because of symmetry about the x-axis. However, in distribution B, because the charge is concentrated closer to the horizontal axis, there is less cancellation of the differential electric field vectors compared to case A. (For further explanation, see Chapter 10.)'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q20', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 20, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'Two cylindrical resistors are arranged in series and connected to a complicated circuit, as shown. Both resistors have the same resistivity, and the second resistor has twice the radius and twice the length of the first resistor. Which of the following statements is correct?', '["The resistors have the same resistance.","The resistors experience the same potential drop.","The resistors dissipate the same amount of power.","The resistors have the same current.","The equivalent resistance of the two resistors is equal to 1.5R2."]'::jsonb, '(D) Recalling that the same current flows through elements in series, you could recognize that choice (D) is correct without paying attention to the details of the problem. The other choices are incorrect for the following reasons. The resistance of a cylindrical resistor is given by the equation R = \rhol/A. Because the second resistor has twice the length and four times the area compared to the first resistor, it has half the resistance of the first resistor, eliminating choice (A). The voltage across the resistors, given by V = IR, is different because the two resistors share the same current but have different resistances, eliminating choice (B). Similarly, power can be expressed as P = I2R, and because the resistors have the same current and different resistances, they dissipate different amounts of power, eliminating choice (D). Finally, R2 is half of R1 (as discussed above), and because resistors in series add, the equivalent resistance is equal to Req = 1.5R1 = 3R2, so choice (E) is incorrect.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q21', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 21, false, 'A', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'In a Wheatstone bridge circuit with four resistors R1, R2, R3, and R4 arranged in two parallel branches (branch 1 has R1 and R2 in series; branch 2 has R3 and R4 in series), the potential at the junction between R1 and R2 equals the potential at the junction between R3 and R4. Which condition must be satisfied?', '["R1 / R2 = R3 / R4","R1 R2 = R3 R4","R1 + R2 = R3 + R4","R1 - R2 = R3 - R4","R1 / R4 = R2 / R3"]'::jsonb, 'For the bridge to be balanced (equal potentials at the two internal nodes), the voltage drop across R1 must equal the voltage drop across R3: I1 R1 = I2 R3, and the voltage drop across R2 must equal that across R4: I1 R2 = I2 R4. Dividing the two equations yields R1 / R2 = R3 / R4 (the classic Wheatstone bridge balance condition).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q22', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 22, false, 'C', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'If each of the capacitors in the network shown has a capacitance of C, what is the equivalent capacitance of the network?', '["C/9","C/3","C","3C","9C"]'::jsonb, '(C) Each set of three capacitors in series has an equivalent capacitance of C/3. Because capacitors in parallel add, the combination of these three equivalent capacitors (each with a capacitance of C/3) in parallel produces a network with an equivalent capacitance of C.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q23', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 23, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'What voltage would have to be imposed across the entire network shown in question 26 for the voltage across any of the capacitors to be V?', '["V/9","V/3","V","3V","9V"]'::jsonb, '(D) Imagine charging the capacitor network. Equal amounts of charge would flow through the three branches of the network, causing equal amounts of charge to build up on every one of the nine capacitors. Therefore, for the voltage of one capacitor to be V, the voltage of every capacitor in the network must be V. When the voltage across each capacitor is V, what is the voltage across the entire network? To calculate the voltage across the network, we can imagine traversing one branch and summing the voltage jumps, as in a Kirchhoff’s loop equation. Because the capacitors have the same polarity, their voltages sum, causing the voltage across the branch (and thus the network) to be 3V.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q24', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 24, false, 'B', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'The gaussian “unit cube” shown lies in a region where the electric field is constant and parallel to the z-axis: . Which of the following choices lists the outward flux through sides 1 through 6, respectively?', '["0, 0, −E, 0, +E, 0","0, 0, +E, 0, −E, 0","0, 0, +E, 0, +E, 0","0, 0, −E, 0, −E, 0","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '(B) Sides 1, 2, 4, and 6 are parallel to the electric field, so the electric flux through them is zero. Sides 3 and 5 are perpendicular to the field, so the magnitude of the flux passing through each of these faces is equal to the area (which is 1) multiplied by the field, E. Because the field points in the positive z-direction, it enters the cube at side 5 (yielding negative outward flux) and leaves the cube at side 3 (yielding positive outward flux).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q25', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 25, false, 'C', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'You have three identical capacitors you are using to design an equivalent capacitor that will be used to store energy for a camera flash, as shown. Charging time is defined as the amount of time required to reach 90% of the maximum energy. Rank the networks in order from least to greatest charging time.', '["I, II, III, IV","IV, III, II, I","I, III, II, IV","IV, II, III, I","I, (II = III), IV I) II) III) IV)"]'::jsonb, '(C) The energy stored in a capacitor can be expressed in the form U = Q2/2C. Therefore, the amount of time required for a capacitor to reach 90% of its maximum energy is equal to the amount of time required to reach 90% of its maximum Q2 value (because C is fixed for any given capacitor). The circuit with the smaller time constant (smaller \tau = RC), and thus the smaller equivalent capacitance, reaches the 90% value more quickly. Therefore, ordering the networks from least to greatest charging time is simply a matter of ordering them from least to greatest equivalence capacitance. The equivalent capacitances of networks I through IV are and 3C, respectively.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q26', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 26, false, 'C', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'Considering the situation introduced in the last problem, rank order the capacitor networks in terms of the energy stored when fully charged. (Assume that the capacitor networks are connected to the same circuit, with a fixed voltage.)', '["I, II, III, IV","IV, III, II, I","I, III, II, IV","IV, II, III, I","I, (II = III), IV"]'::jsonb, '(C) From the equation , networks with higher equivalent capacitance store more energy when connected to the same voltage. Therefore, ordering the capacitors from least to greatest energy is equivalent to ordering them from least to greatest capacitance. This ordering is the same as in question 29, choice (C).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q27', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 27, false, 'E', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'What is the rate at which thermal energy is produced in the resistors shown?', '["(B) IV","Directly downward toward the bottom of the page","2IV","4IV","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '(E) What is the current through the circuit? A Kirchhoff’s loop equation for the circuit (moving in the clockwise direction and choosing positive current clockwise) is V − IR − V − IR = 0, which simplifies to 2IR = 0. Therefore, the current around the circuit is zero, which is not surprising because the two identical batteries are connected so as to oppose each other. Thus, the power dissipated in each of the resistors is zero, and absolutely no thermal energy is produced in the circuit.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q28', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 28, false, 'B', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'In the circuit shown, there is initially no current flowing through the inductor. The instant after the switch is closed, what is the current through the battery?', '["V/R1","V/(R1 + R2)","V/(R1 + R3)","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(B) The instant after the switch is closed in an RL circuit, the inductor acts like an open circuit (because the current through the inductor must be continuous and thus initially zero). Therefore, the equivalent resistance is R1 + R2, and the current is calculated using Ohm’s law with the result given by choice (B). Elimination approach: Even if you have forgotten about RL circuits, you could eliminate enough choices to make a good guess. First, because no circuit elements block the flow of current through resistors R1 and R2, these resistors determine the current and are expected to show up in the final answer, eliminating choices (A) and (C). The denominator of choice (E) has mixed units (Ω + Ω−1), so it must be incorrect. This leaves us to guess between choices (B) and (D).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q29', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 29, false, 'D', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'Referring to the circuit shown in question 32, after the circuit has reached equilibrium, what is the current through the battery?', '["V/R1","V/(R1 + R2)","V/(R1 + R3)","Independent of the mass and charge distribution","Zero net change under static equilibrium conditions"]'::jsonb, '(D) At equilibrium the inductor acts like a short circuit, so to calculate the current we simply ignore the inductor, calculate the equivalent resistance, and apply Ohm’s law. To calculate the equivalent resistance, note that R2 and R3 are in parallel with each other, so their equivalent resistance together is Because these resistors are in series with R1, the equivalent resistance of the entire circuit is Applying Ohm’s law yields the current given in choice (D).'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q30', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 30, false, 'B', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'A solid conducting sphere of radius R carries a net positive charge +Q in electrostatic equilibrium. Taking electric potential to be zero at infinity (V = 0 at r = infinity), which of the following correctly describes the electric potential V(r) as a function of radial distance r from the center?', '["V(r) is zero inside the sphere and proportional to 1/r outside","V(r) is constant and equal to k Q / R for all r <= R, and proportional to k Q / r for r >= R","V(r) increases linearly from 0 at the center to k Q / R at the surface","V(r) is proportional to 1/r^2 both inside and outside the sphere","V(r) is constant throughout all space"]'::jsonb, 'Because the sphere is a conductor in electrostatic equilibrium, the electric field inside is zero (E = 0 for r <= R). Since E = -dV/dr, the potential must be constant throughout the volume of the conductor and equal to its surface value: V(r) = k Q / R for r <= R. Outside the sphere (r >= R), Gauss''s law gives E(r) = k Q / r^2, so integrating from infinity yields V(r) = k Q / r.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-2_q31', 'ap-mock-physc-em-2', 31, false, 'A', 'Practice Test 2 – E&M', 'medium', 'For the solid conducting sphere in the previous question, which of the following correctly describes the electric field magnitude E(r) as a function of radial distance r from the center?', '["E(r) = 0 for r < R, and E(r) = k Q / r^2 for r > R","E(r) is constant and non-zero for r < R","E(r) increases linearly with r for r < R","E(r) is proportional to 1/r for all r","E(r) is non-zero at the center and decreases to 0 at the surface"]'::jsonb, 'In electrostatic equilibrium, charges reside entirely on the outer surface of a conductor, so the electric field inside the conducting material is identically zero: E(r) = 0 for r < R. Outside the sphere (r > R), Gauss''s law with a spherical Gaussian surface enclosing charge Q yields E(4 pi r^2) = Q / epsilon_0 ==> E(r) = k Q / r^2.'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q1', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 1, false, 'A', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/t0317-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent"><strong>Time:</strong> 45 minutes. You may refer to the constants sheet and the equation sheet, both of which are found in the appendix. You may use a calculator.</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0319-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 1 and 2: The circuit above consists of three capacitors and a battery, which has been connected for a long time.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_1" id="ch19r_1">1.</a></strong> What is the charge stored on the 6 nF capacitor?</p>', '["<p>96 nC</p>","<p>48 nC</p>","<p>432 nC</p>","<p>4 nC</p>","<p>144 nC</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_1" id="rch19r_1">1.</a></strong> <strong>A—</strong>First, reduce the circuit to its simplest form. The parallel capacitors add algebraically to get an equivalent capacitance of 12 nF. Then the series capacitors add inversely by 1/$C_{eq}$ = 1/$C_{1}$ + 1/$C_{2}$. Add 1/12 and 1/6 to get 1/4 . . . that means the equivalent capacitance of the circuit is 4 nF. Now by $Q$ = <em>CV</em>, the whole circuit stores (4 nF) × (24 V) = 96 nC. Series capacitors each take the same charge as each other; that is ALSO equal to the total charge across the combination. So the 6 nF capacitor takes all 96 nC.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q2', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 2, false, 'D', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/t0317-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent"><strong>Time:</strong> 45 minutes. You may refer to the constants sheet and the equation sheet, both of which are found in the appendix. You may use a calculator.</p><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0319-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 1 and 2: The circuit above consists of three capacitors and a battery, which has been connected for a long time.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_2" id="ch19r_2">2.</a></strong> What is the voltage across the 8 nF capacitor?</p>', '["<p>2 V</p>","<p>12 V</p>","<p>16 V</p>","<p>8 V</p>","<p>3 V</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_2" id="rch19r_2">2.</a></strong> <strong>D—</strong>The parallel capacitors take the same voltage as each other. Their equivalent capacitance is 12 nF. Both the voltage across this 12 nF equivalent capacitance and the voltage across the 6 nF capacitor must add to 24 V by Kirchhoff’s loop rule. And series capacitors take the same charge in $Q$ = <em>CV</em>. Solving for voltage, the voltage across each capacitor is <em>Q/C</em>, where $Q$ is the same for each. Even if you got the total charge wrong in question 1, you’ll still see that the bigger capacitor takes the smaller voltage, and by a factor of 2 here since 12 nF is twice 6 nF. The 12 nF equivalent capacitor takes 8 V across it (96 nC/12 nF). This means both of the parallel capacitors also take 8 V.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q3', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 3, false, 'C', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0319-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 3 and 4: Two long, straight wires are separated by a distance $d$. The left-hand wire carries a current $I$ directed into the page; the right-hand wire carries a current $I$ directed out of the page. Points R and P are positions in space, located as shown in the diagram.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_3" id="ch19r_3">3.</a></strong> What is the magnitude of the net magnetic field produced at point P?</p>', '["<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0319-03.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0319-04.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0319-05.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0319-06.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0319-07.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_3" id="rch19r_3">3.</a></strong> <strong>C—</strong>The magnetic field due to a long straight wire is $μ_{0}$<em>I/</em>2<em>πr,</em> where $r$ is the distance from the wire to the point being considered. So for the right-hand wire, $r = d$, and for the left-hand wire, $r$ = 2$d$. The direction of the magnetic field is given by the second right-hand rule: point the thumb with the current, curl the fingers to show the field direction. That’s down the page due to the right-hand wire, up the page due to the left-hand wire. So the magnitudes of the two magnetic fields subtract to the total: $μ_{0}$<em>I/</em>2<em>πd</em> − $μ_{0}$<em>I/</em>2$\pi$(2$d$) gives choice C.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q4', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 4, false, 'B', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0319-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 3 and 4: Two long, straight wires are separated by a distance $d$. The left-hand wire carries a current $I$ directed into the page; the right-hand wire carries a current $I$ directed out of the page. Points R and P are positions in space, located as shown in the diagram.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_4" id="ch19r_4">4.</a></strong> What is the direction of the net magnetic field produced at point R?</p>', '["<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/larrow.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p>Zero</p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/2arrow.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/rarrow.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/4arrow.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_4" id="rch19r_4">4.</a></strong> <strong>B—</strong>Use the second right-hand rule: point the thumb with the current, and the fingers show the magnetic field direction. The left-hand wire produces a field down and to the right. The right-hand wire produces a field up and to the left. These fields are of equal magnitude, because the currents are the same and the distances from the wires are the same. So the magnetic fields subtract to a net of zero.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q5', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 5, false, 'C', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0320-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 5 and 6: A proton initially moving right with speed $v$ enters a region in which the magnetic field is uniform. The proton is deflected along the path shown, exiting the magnetic field at position X. Next, an electron initially moving right with identical speed $v$ enters the magnetic field from the same position.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_5" id="ch19r_5">5.</a></strong> Which of the labeled positions is closest to where the electron will exit the magnetic field?</p>', '["<p>A</p>","<p>B</p>","<p>C</p>","<p>D</p>","<p>X</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_5" id="rch19r_5">5.</a></strong> <strong>C—</strong>Apply net$F$ = <em>ma</em> in circular motion. Here the net force on the object is <em>qvB</em>, and the acceleration in a circular path is $v^{2}$/$r$. Solve for $r$ to get $r$ = <em>mv/qB</em>. The electron and proton experience the same magnetic field $B$, the same speed $v$, and have the same amount of charge $q$. The difference is, the mass of a proton is something like 2000 times that of the electron. So the radius of the proton’s circular motion will be 2000 times bigger. The only position close to showing an orbital radius 2000 times smaller for the electron is C.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q6', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 6, false, 'B', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0320-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 5 and 6: A proton initially moving right with speed $v$ enters a region in which the magnetic field is uniform. The proton is deflected along the path shown, exiting the magnetic field at position X. Next, an electron initially moving right with identical speed $v$ enters the magnetic field from the same position.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_6" id="ch19r_6">6.</a></strong> The speed of the proton a very short time $t$ after entering the magnetic field is $v_{p}$; the speed of the electron the same time $t$ after entering the magnetic field is $v_{e}$. Which of the following correctly compares $v_{p}$ and $v_{e}$?</p>', '["<p>$v_{p}$ is smaller than $v_{e}$ by more than a factor of 10</p>","<p>$v_{p}$ is equal to $v_{e}$</p>","<p>$v_{p}$ is larger than $v_{e}$ by less than a factor of 10</p>","<p>$v_{p}$ is larger than $v_{e}$ by more than a factor of 10</p>","<p>$v_{p}$ is smaller than $v_{e}$ by less than a factor of 10</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_6" id="rch19r_6">6.</a></strong> <strong>B—</strong>The direction of a magnetic force is always perpendicular to the velocity vector. This means that the magnetic force causes circular motion, but not a change in speed, for an object in the field. The magnetic field won’t change the speed of “any” particle.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q7', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 7, false, 'B', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0320-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 7 and 8: A capacitor $C_{1}$ is initially connected to a 12 V battery for a long time, as in diagram 1. Next, in diagram 2 the battery is disconnected, and in diagram 3 $C_{1}$ is connected instead to capacitor $C_{2}$, which has an initial charge.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_7" id="ch19r_7">7.</a></strong> Which of the following must be identical quantities in diagram 3?</p><p class="roman1t">I. The charge on each capacitor</p><p class="roman2">II. The voltage across each capacitor</p><p class="roman3">III. The capacitance of each capacitor</p>', '["<p>I only</p>","<p>II only</p>","<p>III only</p>","<p>I and II only</p>","<p>I, II, and III</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_7" id="rch19r_7">7.</a></strong> <strong>B—</strong>Kirchoff’s loop rule indicates that since this a closed circuit loop, the voltage across each must be the same. Capacitance is a property of a capacitor, so the capacitances can be different regardless of what circuit they’re hooked up to. Then by $Q$ = <em>CV</em> the charge can be different if the capacitances are different, even with the same voltages.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q8', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 8, false, 'C', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0320-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 7 and 8: A capacitor $C_{1}$ is initially connected to a 12 V battery for a long time, as in diagram 1. Next, in diagram 2 the battery is disconnected, and in diagram 3 $C_{1}$ is connected instead to capacitor $C_{2}$, which has an initial charge.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_8" id="ch19r_8">8.</a></strong> Which of the following must be identical quantities for capacitor $C_{1}$ in circuits 1 and 3?</p><p class="roman1t">I. The voltage across $C_{1}$</p><p class="roman2">II. The charge on $C_{1}$</p><p class="roman3">III. The capacitance of $C_{1}$</p>', '["<p>I only</p>","<p>II only</p>","<p>III only</p>","<p>I and II only</p>","<p>I, II, and III</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_8" id="rch19r_8">8.</a></strong> <strong>C—</strong>Capacitance is a property of a capacitor. It’s the same capacitor in both diagrams, so it has the same capacitance. Charge is free to flow between capacitors in diagram 3, so the charge on $C_{1}$ will change. And while the voltage across each capacitor in diagram 3 must be the same for each, that doesn’t have to be 12 V and probably will not be.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q9', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 9, false, 'A', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0321-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 9 and 10 refer to the circuit above.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_9" id="ch19r_9">9.</a></strong> What is the resistance of $R_{2}$?</p>', '["<p>33 Ω</p>","<p>3 Ω</p>","<p>150 Ω</p>","<p>100 Ω</p>","<p>20 Ω</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_9" id="rch19r_9">9.</a></strong> <strong>A—</strong>The voltage across the middle branch of the circuit is <em>IR</em> = (2 A)(50 Ω) = 100 V. That’s the same as the voltage across $R_{2}$, because parallel resistors take the same voltage as each other. The current through $R_{2}$ is 3 A by Kirchoff’s junction rule (5 A enters the junction, 2 A goes down the middle, so the remaining 3 A goes through $R_{2}$). Set 100 V = (3 A)($R_{2}$) and solve for $R_{2}$ = 33 Ω.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q10', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 10, false, 'A', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<div class="passage-box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-left: 3px solid #7c3aed;"><p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0321-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="noindent">Questions 9 and 10 refer to the circuit above.</p></div><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_10" id="ch19r_10">10.</a></strong> What is the voltage measured by the voltmeter?</p>', '["<p>200 V</p>","<p>20 V</p>","<p>60 V</p>","<p>100 V</p>","<p>250 V</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_10" id="rch19r_10">10.</a></strong> <strong>A—</strong>Use Kirchoff’s loop rule. The battery provides 300 V. So the voltage drop across $R_{1}$ and the 50 Ω resistor must also be 300 V. The voltage across the 50 Ω resistor is (2 A)(50 Ω) = 100 V. So the remaining 200 V must be across $R_{1}$, which is what the voltmeter measures.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q11', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 11, false, 'C', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0321-02.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_11" id="ch19r_11">11.</a></strong> Four parallel-plate capacitors had different plate diameters $d$ and distances $x$ between the plates, as shown above. Which of the following ranks the capacitors according to their capacitance?</p>', '["<p>C = B &gt; A &gt; D</p>","<p>C &gt; A &gt; D &gt; B</p>","<p>C &gt; D &gt; A &gt; B</p>","<p>D &gt; C = B &gt; A</p>","<p>D &gt; C &gt; B &gt; A</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_11" id="rch19r_11">11.</a></strong> <strong>C—</strong>The capacitance of a parallel plate capacitor is <em>ɛ</em><sub>0</sub>$A$/$d$, where $A$ is the plate area and $d$ is the distance between plates. Here that means the capacitance is proportional to $d^{2}$/$x$. Evaluating each capacitor according to $d^{2}$/$x$, capacitor C has largest capacitance of (2$d$ )<sup>2</sup><em>/x</em> = 4($d^{2}$/$x$). Capacitor D works out to 2($d^{2}$/$x$), capacitor A to 1($d^{2}$/$x$), and capacitor B to (1/2)($d^{2}$/$x$).</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q12', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 12, false, 'A', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_12" id="ch19r_12">12.</a></strong> A parallel plate capacitor has capacitance $C$. Each plate of the capacitor holds a charge of magnitude $Q$. Which of the following expressions gives the energy stored in this capacitor?</p>', '["<p>½$Q^{2}$/$C$</p>","<p>$Q^{2}$/$C$</p>","<p><em>QC</em></p>","<p>½<em>QC</em><sup>2</sup></p>","<p>½<em>Q/C</em></p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_12" id="rch19r_12">12.</a></strong> <strong>A—</strong>The standard expression for energy stored on a capacitor is ½<em>CV</em><sup>2</sup>. But the fundamental equation for capacitors says that $Q$ = <em>CV</em>. So, substitute $V$ = <em>Q/C</em> into the standard expression. You’re left with ½$Q^{2}$/$C$.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q13', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 13, false, 'A', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<p class="image"><img alt="Images" class="q-img" src="images/f0322-01.jpg" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;"/></p><p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_13" id="ch19r_13">13.</a></strong> In region 1, a uniform electric field of magnitude $E_{1}$ points right. In region 2, another uniform electric field of smaller magnitude $E_{2}$ points to the left, as shown above. A positive charge $+q$ is released from rest at the position indicated, at the far left of region 1. Which of the following graphs shows the speed $v$ of the charge as a function of time?</p>', '["<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0322-02.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0322-03.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0322-04.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0322-05.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>","<p><img alt=\"Images\" class=\"q-img\" src=\"images/f0322-06.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0; display: block;\"/></p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_13" id="rch19r_13">13.</a></strong> <strong>A—</strong>The force on the charge is <em>qE</em> and thus is uniform within each region. A uniform force produces a uniform acceleration, and thus a uniform slope on a velocity-time graph. The graph must be straight in each region. In region 1, $E$ is larger, so the force, the acceleration, and the slope of the velocity-time graph will be larger as well.</p>'),
  ('ap-mock-physc-em-3_q14', 'ap-mock-physc-em-3', 14, false, 'C', 'AP Physics C: Electricity & Magnetism', 'medium', '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19v.xhtml#rch19r_14" id="ch19r_14">14.</a></strong> Consider a long, straight wire of circular cross section. The current through the wire has uniform current density across its cross-sectional area. Let the distance from the center of the wire along a cross-sectional slice be $r$. The magnetic field inside the wire is proportional to which of the following?</p>', '["<p>$r^{2}$</p>","<p>$r$<sup>1/2</sup></p>","<p>$r$</p>","<p>$r^{-}\\$2</p>","<p>$r^{-}\\$1</p>"]'::jsonb, '<p class="ques"><strong><a href="ch19r.xhtml#ch19r_14" id="rch19r_14">14.</a></strong> <strong>C—</strong>The symmetry is appropriate for Ampere’s law. Never evaluate the integral in Ampere’s law—it always simplifies to <em>BL</em> = $μ_{0} I_{enclosed}$. Here $L$ is the circumference of an amperian loop, 2<em>πr</em>. The current enclosed by this loop is a fraction of the total current. What fraction? A fraction equal to the area enclosed by the amperian loop over the total cross-sectional area of the wire. That’s <em>πr</em><sup>2</sup> over the total area of the wire. Point is, Ampere’s law looks like $B$(2<em>πr</em>) = $μ_{0}$(<em>πr</em><sup>2</sup>/total area)(total $I$ ). Solving for $B$, you’re left with an expression with $r$ by itself in the numerator. So the magnetic field is proportional to $r$. (You’ve memorized the equation $B = μ_{0} I$/2<em>πr</em>, but that’s for the magnetic field <em>outside</em> a long straight wire. This question is asking about $B$ inside the wire.)</p>')
