  $$a = \frac{|q|E}{m} = \frac{(5 \times 10^{-13}\text{ C})(50\text{ N/C})}{10^{-10}\text{ kg}} = \frac{2.5 \times 10^{-11}}{10^{-10}} = 0.25\text{ m/s}^2$$
  $$d = \frac{1}{2}at^2 \implies t = \sqrt{\frac{2d}{a}} = \sqrt{\frac{2(0.30\text{ m})}{0.25\text{ m/s}^2}} = \sqrt{2.4} \approx 1.55\text{ s}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-mastery_q3', 'ap-topic-physc-em-mastery', 3, false, 'D', 'Electric Field Superposition & Tripling', 'medium', 'Points A and B are equidistant from point P. At A, a charge $+Q$ creates field $\vec{E}$ pointing to the right at P. What charge placed at B will triple the net electric field at P?', '["$+2Q$","$+3Q$","$-Q$","$-2Q$","$-3Q$"]'::jsonb, '(D) To amplify the field at P to $3E$ in the same direction, the charge at B must contribute an electric field of magnitude $2E$ directed toward B (to the right). A negative charge attracts a positive test charge, so the charge must be negative with twice the magnitude: $q_B = -2Q$.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-mastery_q4', 'ap-topic-physc-em-mastery', 4, false, 'D', 'Repulsive Coulomb Force Kinematics', 'easy', 'A fixed sphere $+Q$ repels a small movable sphere $+q$ released from rest. The small sphere moves away with:', '["decreasing velocity and decreasing acceleration","decreasing velocity and increasing acceleration","decreasing velocity and constant acceleration","increasing velocity and decreasing acceleration","increasing velocity and increasing acceleration"]'::jsonb, '(D) As $r$ increases, the repulsive force $F = \frac{kQq}{r^2}$ and acceleration $a = F/m$ decrease toward zero. Because acceleration remains strictly positive ($a > 0$), the speed and velocity continue to increase ($dv/dt > 0$).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-mastery_q5', 'ap-topic-physc-em-mastery', 5, false, 'A', 'Electrostatic Potential Energy Turnaround', 'hard', 'A particle of charge $q = 1\ \mu\text{C}$ is fixed at the origin. An object ($m = 10^{-3}\text{ kg}, q = 1\ \mu\text{C}$) is released from rest $1\text{ m}$ directly above it. How far does the object fall before coming momentarily to rest ($g = 10\text{ m/s}^2$)?', '["$0.1\\text{ m}$","$0.3\\text{ m}$","$0.5\\text{ m}$","$0.7\\text{ m}$","$0.9\\text{ m}$"]'::jsonb, '(A) At the turnaround point (fall distance $x$), $\Delta K = 0$, so net work done by gravity and electrostatic repulsion is zero:
  $$W_g + W_E = 0 \implies mgx - k q^2 \left(\frac{1}{1-x} - 1\right) = 0 \implies mgx = \frac{k q^2 x}{1-x}$$
  $$1 - x = \frac{k q^2}{mg} = \frac{(9 \times 10^9)(10^{-6})^2}{(10^{-3})(10)} = \frac{9 \times 10^{-3}}{10^{-2}} = 0.9 \implies x = 1 - 0.9 = 0.1\text{ m}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-mastery_q6', 'ap-topic-physc-em-mastery', 6, false, 'B', 'Gauss''s Law Flux vs. Point Field Superposition', 'medium', 'Charges $Q_2, Q_3$ are inside a Gaussian surface while $Q_1, Q_4$ are outside. Point P is on the surface. Which is true?', '["Flux depends on all four; $\\vec{E}$ at P depends on $Q_2, Q_3$.","Flux depends only on $Q_2, Q_3$; $\\vec{E}$ at P depends on all four.","Flux depends on $Q_2, Q_3$; $\\vec{E}$ at P depends on $Q_1, Q_2, Q_3$.","Flux depends on $Q_1, Q_4$; $\\vec{E}$ at P depends on $Q_2, Q_3$.","Both flux and $\\vec{E}$ depend on all four."]'::jsonb, '(B) By Gauss''s Law, total enclosed flux $\Phi_E = \frac{Q_{\text{enc}}}{\varepsilon_0} = \frac{Q_2 + Q_3}{\varepsilon_0}$ depends only on internal charges. By the principle of linear superposition, the local electric field vector $\vec{E}$ at any spatial point P is the vector sum of fields produced by all charges present ($Q_1, Q_2, Q_3, Q_4$).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-mastery_q7', 'ap-topic-physc-em-mastery', 7, false, 'A', 'Uniformly Charged Sphere Interior Field', 'hard', 'A nonconducting solid sphere of radius $R$ carries uniform charge $-Q$. What is the electric field magnitude at distance $r < R$ from the center?', '["$\\frac{Q r}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R^3}$","$\\frac{Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R^2}$","$\\frac{Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R^3}$","$\\frac{Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 R^3 r}$","$\\frac{Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^2}$"]'::jsonb, '(A) Enclosed charge at radius $r < R$: $Q_{\text{enc}} = -Q\left(\frac{r^3}{R^3}\right)$. Applying Gauss''s Law:
  $$E(4\pi r^2) = \frac{Q(r^3/R^3)}{\varepsilon_0} \implies E(r) = \frac{Q r}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 R^3}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-mastery_q8', 'ap-topic-physc-em-mastery', 8, false, 'A', 'Gaussian Surface Enclosing Electric Dipole', 'easy', 'Calculate the electric flux through a Gaussian surface enclosing an electric dipole of charge magnitude $q$.', '["$0$","$\\frac{Aq}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}$","$\\frac{Aq^2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}$","$\\frac{Aq}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r}$","$\\frac{Aq}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^2}$"]'::jsonb, '(A) A dipole consists of equal and opposite charges $+q$ and $-q$. Total enclosed net charge $Q_{\text{enc}} = +q + (-q) = 0$. By Gauss''s Law, $\Phi_E = \frac{Q_{\text{enc}}}{\varepsilon_0} = 0$.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-mastery_q9', 'ap-topic-physc-em-mastery', 9, false, 'C', 'Equipotential Work & Potential Difference', 'medium', 'An electron moves from point A ($-10\text{ V}$) to E ($-10\text{ V}$), then from E to C ($0\text{ V}$). Describe the work done by the electric field for each step.', '["Negative; Positive","Zero; Positive","Zero; Negative","Positive; Zero","Positive; Positive"]'::jsonb, '(C) From A to E: $\Delta V = 0\text{ V}$ (equipotential line), so $W_E = -q\Delta V = 0\text{ J}$.  
From E to C: $\Delta V = 0 - (-10) = +10\text{ V}$. Work done by the electric field on the negative electron: $W_E = -q\Delta V = -(-e)(+10\text{ V}) = -10\text{ eV}$ (negative work).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-mastery_q10', 'ap-topic-physc-em-mastery', 10, false, 'B', 'Work by Conservative Electric Field', 'easy', 'How much work does the electric field do moving charge $q = -0.05\text{ C}$ from $V_A = 200\text{ V}$ to $V_B = 100\text{ V}$ along a curved path?', '["$-5\\text{ J}$","$+5\\text{ J}$","$-10\\text{ J}$","$+10\\text{ J}$","$0\\text{ J}$"]'::jsonb, '(B) $$W_E = -q(V_B - V_A) = -(-0.05\text{ C})(100\text{ V} - 200\text{ V}) = +0.05(-100) \implies W_E = -\Delta U = +5\text{ J}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-mastery_q11', 'ap-topic-physc-em-mastery', 11, false, 'E', 'Capacitor Dielectric Insertion & Stored Energy', 'easy', 'A capacitor is fully charged by a battery, disconnected, and then a dielectric is inserted. Which statements are true?  I. Voltage remains the same.  II. Potential energy increases.  III. Capacitance increases.', '["I only","I and II only","I and III only","II and III only","III only"]'::jsonb, '(E) With the battery disconnected, stored charge $Q$ is constant. Inserting a dielectric increases capacitance ($C = \kappa C_0$, III is true). Voltage decreases ($V = Q/C$, I is false), and stored energy decreases ($U = Q^2/(2C)$, II is false).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-mastery_q12', 'ap-topic-physc-em-mastery', 12, false, 'C', 'Electric Potential Energy Sign Definition', 'easy', 'If the electric field does negative work on a negative charge moving from A to B, the electrical potential energy:', '["is negative","is positive","increases","decreases","cannot be determined"]'::jsonb, '(C) By definition, $\Delta U_E = -W_E$. If $W_E < 0$, then $\Delta U_E > 0$, meaning the potential energy increases.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-mastery_q13', 'ap-topic-physc-em-mastery', 13, false, 'D', 'Parallel Capacitors Individual Stored Energy', 'easy', 'Three $6\ \mu\text{F}$ capacitors are connected in parallel across a $9\text{ V}$ battery. Determine the energy stored in each individual capacitor.', '["$2.43\\text{ J}$","$7.29 \\times 10^{-4}\\text{ J}$","$8.10 \\times 10^{-5}\\text{ J}$","$2.43 \\times 10^{-4}\\text{ J}$","$27\\text{ J}$"]'::jsonb, '(D) Each parallel capacitor receives the full $9\text{ V}$ potential difference:
  $$U = \frac{1}{2}C V^2 = \frac{1}{2}(6 \times 10^{-6}\text{ F})(9\text{ V})^2 = (3 \times 10^{-6})(81) = 2.43 \times 10^{-4}\text{ J}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-mastery_q14', 'ap-topic-physc-em-mastery', 14, false, 'C', 'Equipotential Surfaces in Uniform Field', 'easy', 'In a uniform electric field between parallel plates, which points lie on the same equipotential?', '["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 4 only","3 and 4 only","All points lie on same equipotential"]'::jsonb, '(C) Equipotential surfaces in a uniform electric field are parallel planes perpendicular to the field lines. Points located at the same perpendicular distance from the plates share the identical electric potential.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-mastery_q15', 'ap-topic-physc-em-mastery', 15, false, 'C', 'Connected Conducting Spheres Charge Equilibrium', 'medium', 'Isolated conducting spheres of radius $a$ (Sphere 1) and $4a$ (Sphere 2) each carry charge $-Q$. Connected by a thin wire, what are the final charges?', '["$-Q,\\ -Q$","$-2Q/3,\\ -4Q/3$","$-2Q/5,\\ -8Q/5$","$-4Q/5,\\ -6Q/5$","$-Q/2,\\ -3Q/2$"]'::jsonb, '(C) Total charge $Q_{\text{tot}} = -2Q$. When connected, potential equalizes: $V_1 = V_2 \implies \frac{k q_1}{a} = \frac{k q_2}{4a} \implies q_2 = 4q_1$.
  $$q_1 + 4q_1 = -2Q \implies 5q_1 = -2Q \implies q_1 = -\frac{2}{5}Q,\quad q_2 = -\frac{8}{5}Q$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-mastery_q16', 'ap-topic-physc-em-mastery', 16, false, 'A', 'Resistivity & Conductor Cross-Section Ratio', 'easy', 'Brass wire has $4\times$ diameter and $5\times$ resistivity of silver wire of the same length. Compare resistances $R_B$ and $R_S$.', '["$R_B = \\frac{5}{16}R_S$","$R_B = \\frac{5}{4}R_S$","$R_B = \\frac{5}{64}R_S$","$R_B = \\frac{16}{5}R_S$","$R_B = 20 R_S$"]'::jsonb, '(A) Area scales with diameter squared: $A_B = 4^2 A_S = 16 A_S$.
  $$R_B = \frac{\rho_B L}{A_B} = \frac{(5\rho_S)L}{16 A_S} = \frac{5}{16}R_S$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-mastery_q17', 'ap-topic-physc-em-mastery', 17, false, 'D', 'Ohm''s Law Proportionality', 'easy', 'Doubling the voltage across an ohmic conductor without changing resistance causes current to:', '["decrease by 4","decrease by 2","remain unchanged","increase by a factor of 2","increase by 4"]'::jsonb, '(D) By Ohm''s Law, $I = V/R$. For constant $R$, current is directly proportional to voltage, doubling when $V$ is doubled.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-mastery_q18', 'ap-topic-physc-em-mastery', 18, false, 'C', 'Circuit Power & Operating Voltage', 'easy', 'A circuit with total resistance $5\ \Omega$ dissipates $125\text{ W}$ of power. At what voltage is it operating?', '["$5\\text{ V}$","$15\\text{ V}$","$25\\text{ V}$","$45\\text{ V}$","$625\\text{ V}$"]'::jsonb, '(C) $$P = \frac{V^2}{R} \implies V = \sqrt{P R} = \sqrt{(125\text{ W})(5\ \Omega)} = \sqrt{625} = 25\text{ V}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-mastery_q19', 'ap-topic-physc-em-mastery', 19, false, 'A', 'Incandescent Bulb Overvoltage Power', 'easy', 'A $120\text{ V}, 60\text{ W}$ bulb is connected to a $240\text{ V}$ outlet. How bright is the bulb compared to standard operation?', '["4 times as bright","2 times as bright","Same brightness","Half as bright","One-quarter as bright"]'::jsonb, '(A) $$P = \frac{V^2}{R} \propto V^2$$
Doubling the operating voltage quadruples the power dissipated ($2^2 = 4$), making the bulb 4 times as bright.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-mastery_q20', 'ap-topic-physc-em-mastery', 20, false, 'B', 'Ammeter Construction & Series Resistance', 'easy', 'An ammeter arranged to measure circuit branch current should possess which characteristics?', '["High resistance, in series","Low resistance, in series","High resistance, in parallel","Low resistance, in parallel","Same resistance, in series"]'::jsonb, '(B) Ammeters must be placed in series so the entire branch current passes through them. Their internal resistance must approach zero ($R \approx 0$) so they do not add series resistance or alter the circuit current.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-mastery_q21', 'ap-topic-physc-em-mastery', 21, false, 'A', 'Magnetic Force Work & Trajectory Properties', 'easy', 'Which statements are true regarding magnetic forces?  I. Magnetic force can never do work on a charged particle.  II. Magnetic force can never change the velocity of a charged particle.  III. A charged particle always experiences a force moving in a magnetic field.', '["I only","I and II only","II and III only","III only","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '(A) $\vec{F}_B = q(\vec{v}\times\vec{B})$ is always perpendicular to $\vec{v}$, so power $P = \vec{F}_B\cdot\vec{v} = 0$ and work is always zero (I is true). It alters direction, thus changing velocity vector $\vec{v}$ (II is false). Moving parallel to $\vec{B}$ produces zero force (III is false).'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-mastery_q22', 'ap-topic-physc-em-mastery', 22, false, 'C', 'Magnetic Lorentz Force & Particle Acceleration', 'medium', 'Particle ($q = 4.0\text{ nC}, m = 2 \times 10^{-4}\text{ kg}$) moves perpendicular to $B = 0.1\text{ T}$ at $3 \times 10^4\text{ m/s}$. What is its acceleration?', '["$0.0006\\text{ m/s}^2$","$0.006\\text{ m/s}^2$","$0.06\\text{ m/s}^2$","$0.6\\text{ m/s}^2$","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '(C) $$F_B = q v B = (4.0 \times 10^{-9}\text{ C})(3 \times 10^4\text{ m/s})(0.1\text{ T}) = 1.2 \times 10^{-5}\text{ N}$$
$$a = \frac{F_B}{m} = \frac{1.2 \times 10^{-5}\text{ N}}{2 \times 10^{-4}\text{ kg}} = 0.06\text{ m/s}^2$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-mastery_q23', 'ap-topic-physc-em-mastery', 23, false, 'D', 'Biot-Savart Law Straight Wire Magnetic Field', 'easy', 'Find the magnetic field $2\text{ cm}$ from a long straight wire carrying $10\text{ A}$.', '["$1 \\times 10^{-6}\\text{ T}$","$1 \\times 10^{-5}\\text{ T}$","$2 \\times 10^{-5}\\text{ T}$","$1 \\times 10^{-4}\\text{ T}$","$2 \\times 10^{-4}\\text{ T}$"]'::jsonb, '(D) $$B = \frac{\mu_0 I}{2\pi r} = \frac{(4\pi \times 10^{-7})(10)}{2\pi (0.02)} = \frac{4 \times 10^{-6}}{0.04} = 1.0 \times 10^{-4}\text{ T}$$'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-mastery_q24', 'ap-topic-physc-em-mastery', 24, false, 'E', 'Motional EMF & Endpoint Potential Polarity', 'medium', 'A metal rod of length $L$ is pulled upward with speed $v$ through a uniform magnetic field $B$ pointing out of the page. What is the potential difference between endpoints $a$ (left) and $b$ (right)?', '["$0$","$\\frac{1}{2}vBL$, $a$ higher","$\\frac{1}{2}vBL$, $b$ higher","$vBL$, point $a$ at higher potential","$vBL$, point $b$ at higher potential"]'::jsonb, '(E) By the right-hand rule, $\vec{v}\times\vec{B}$ for upward velocity and out-of-page field points to the right. Mobile electrons are pushed left toward point $a$, leaving positive charge accumulation at point $b$. Point $b$ is at the higher electric potential by $\Delta V = vBL$.'),
  ('ap-topic-physc-em-mastery_q25', 'ap-topic-physc-em-mastery', 25, false, 'A', 'Motional EMF on Rails & Lenz''s Law Current', 'medium', 'A conducting rod of length $0.2\text{ m}$ and resistance $10\ \Omega$ slides at $3\text{ m/s}$ to the left along rails in $B = 0.5\text{ T}$ directed into the page. What is the magnitude and direction of the induced current?', '["$0.03\\text{ A}$, downward","$0.03\\text{ A}$, upward","$0.3\\text{ A}$, downward","$0.3\\text{ A}$, upward","$0.015\\text{ A}$, downward"]'::jsonb, '(A) $$\mathcal{E} = B L v = (0.5\text{ T})(0.2\text{ m})(3\text{ m/s}) = 0.3\text{ V}$$
$$I = \frac{\mathcal{E}}{R} = \frac{0.3\text{ V}}{10\ \Omega} = 0.03\text{ A}$$
Moving to the left decreases the loop area and inward flux. By Lenz''s Law, the induced current circulates clockwise to produce inward flux, flowing downward through the moving rod.')
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

